  "raw": {
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Assistant-Director--Online-Faculty-Relations--Rabb-School_R0013766-1": {
      "title": "Assistant Director, Online Faculty Relations, Rabb School",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-13\nFull time\n\nBrandeis University is delighted to announce a career opportunity as the Assistant Director, Online Faculty Relations, Rabb School. At Brandeis, we offer a competitive benefits and compensation package which includes medical, dental and life insurances.  If you are looking to advance your career through educational opportunities, Brandeis offers tuition assistance for employees, dependents and spouses.  Our 403b retirement savings plan includes a generous match. The Assistant Director of Online Faculty Relations oversees talent management and facilitates faculty development within the Rabb School at Brandeis University. This includes assessing and maintaining teaching capacity across programs, defining hiring plans, ensuring policy compliance, and managing communications for instructors, teaching assistants, program chairs and advisory council members. Additionally, the Assistant Director focuses on online faculty talent development and is responsible for collecting information to evaluate teaching effectiveness, reviewing that information with program chairs, and communicating constructive feedback to faculty in a timely manner. This individual also collaborates with internal and university-wide stakeholders to offer a robust collection of online faculty development opportunities, resources and tools . The hiring range for this position is $70k - $72k. This is a hybrid position; Wednesday in the office. Please submit a resume and cover letter to be considered for this position. Key Responsibilities: Talent Management Project and maintain appropriate level of teaching capacity and talent needs across new and existing Rabb programs, including instructors, teaching assistants, program chairs, and program advisory council members. Define hiring plans and oversee the hiring process for instructors, teaching assistants, program chairs, and program advisory council members. Partner with the Provost’s Faculty Affairs team to effectively develop and manage contracts for online faculty, online program chairs and teaching assistants following the appropriate Union and Universities policies. Assign instructors to courses in accordance with union requirements. Collaborate and coordinate with the Provost’s Faculty Affairs team to ensure compensation for instructors, teaching assistants, and program chairs is processed in a timely and accurate manner. Facilitate with faculty, teaching assistants and programs chairs to help mediate when teaching and/or classroom management challenges arise throughout the term. Monitor and recommend contract renewal/non-renewal and multi-year contract decisions for online faculty and program chairs. Ensure all practices and policies are in compliance with the Part-Time Faculty Collective Bargaining Agreement. Direct communications to all new and continuing instructors, teaching assistants, program chairs, and advisory council members. Manage essential instructor resources, including schedules, calendars, handbooks, university and school-wide policies and procedures, etc. Manage faculty and program chair involvement in course design and course refresh projects. Facilitate with program chairs annual faculty meetings, providing thought partnership on topics to cover as well as scheduling support. Assist with the departmental budget process, tracking faculty teaching and course development-related costs and projecting future compensation costs for future academic years. Resolve issues, interpret policies, and develop and refine services and processes. Performs all other duties and responsibilities as assigned or directed by the supervisor. Online Faculty Talent Development Design and deliver orientation for new online instructors, teaching assistants, and program chairs. Collaborate with Rabb’s academic leadership, Learning Design Team and the Center for Teaching & Learning to develop and implement a robust, ongoing program of online faculty development opportunities, resources and tools. Conduct course audits to evaluate teaching effectiveness and instructional quality for instructors and teaching assistants throughout the session and support course reviews as needed. Partner with Institutional Research to manage the mid-course and end-of-course evaluation processes, deliver actionable insights for academic quality improvement. Orchestrate with Program Chairs to review faculty performance metrics and disseminate feedback to instructors in a timely and constructive manner. Conduct exit interviews and analyze data to identify patterns and root causes of faculty departures. Analyze factors contributing to faculty turnover and develop retention strategies to mitigate attrition. Monitor satisfaction levels among instructors, program chairs, and teaching assistants and proactively address issues to enhance overall job satisfaction and engagement. Create communication campaigns that support teaching excellence throughout the session life-cycle and create community among instructors. Scheduling Collaborate with internal stakeholders to coordinate faculty and program chair involvement in marketing and program promotion initiatives, mentorship opportunities, course scheduling decisions and other departmental projects/needs. Assess course schedules to ensure they maximize per-course enrollments and minimize course cancellations. Determine course schedule adjustments during the registration period in collaboration with colleagues from admissions and student services. Education & Work Experience: Master’s degree required. Doctorate preferred. 3+ years online higher education teaching, 3+ years of experience in faculty services, talent development/recruitment, HR or higher education administration, experience working and supporting asynchronous online graduate programs and adjunct faculty in higher education and/or with union. Qualifications: Able to establish strong relationships and collaboratively with faculty, colleagues, and other stakeholders across the university. Proficiency in software applications such as Microsoft Word, Excel, Google spreadsheets, and Smartsheets, etc. Experience using Learning Management Systems. Moodle preferred. Budget management experience a plus. Experience working with a CRM, preference with Slate. Experience with faculty and student information system (Workday). Able to function independently and as part of a team. Clear, effective, and diplomatic communication skills. Able to demonstrate sound judgment and initiative in solving problems. Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Assistant-Director--Graduate-Admissions_R0013773-1": {
      "title": "Assistant Director, Graduate Admissions",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-13\nFull time\n\nBrandeis University is delighted to announce a career opportunity as the Assistant Director, Graduate Admissions. At Brandeis, we offer a competitive benefits and compensation package which includes medical, dental and life insurances.  If you are looking to advance your career through educational opportunities, Brandeis offers tuition assistance for employees, dependents and spouses.  Our 403b retirement savings plan includes a generous match.   The Assistant Director of Graduate Admissions is a key recruitment team member tasked with building brand awareness and delivering exceptional customer service to qualified prospective students, leading to application submission and ultimately graduate program enrollment. Reporting to the Associate Director, Graduate Admissions, the Assistant Director will conduct significant outbound phone, texting, social media and email work to prospective candidates; staff recruiting events (including virtual events as well as in-person recruitment events domestically and/or internationally); hold candidate interviews and appointments; read applications and manage initial yield questions around scholarships and financing when applicable.   The hiring range for this position is $62k - $66k.   Responsibilities: Converts inquiries to applicants and ultimately enrolled students through proactive and responsive consultative sales outreach via phone, email, and/or digital platforms. Schedules and conducts candidate interviews and/or appointments and uses a consultative sales approach during these sessions to understand candidate’s decision-making factors; tailors outreach and follow-up to address these decision-making factors in order to improve yield. Promotes Brandeis University and conducts on and off campus presentations via in-house admissions events (virtual and in person) and recruitment travel as needed (both domestic and international) and plays a key role in planning and managing these events. With an eye towards exemplary customer service, provides subject-matter expertise on a suite of graduate programs, while engaging in internal meetings with faculty, staff and students across the full graduate school portfolio to ensure high-level awareness of all Brandeis graduate programs. Reads applications for admission and makes informed recommendations on admissibility. Drafts compelling emails with clear action-items that can be leveraged as slate templates and/or ad hoc recruitment emails when needed. Assumes additional responsibilities as needed. Education and Work Experience: Bachelor's degree required. 2+ years of related experience.   Requirements: Ability to travel to recruitment fairs both domestically and internationally. Valid driver’s license and passport required. Excellent communication skills; ability to represent the graduate programs within and outside Brandeis and comfortable with outbound phone work and presentations. Creative and proactive team member who is excited to build new, scalable processes alongside other admissions team members. Hybrid work environment Excitement and ability to travel to recruitment fairs both domestically and internationally; valid driver’s license and passport. Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Staff-Psychologist_R0013473-1": {
      "title": "Staff Psychologist",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-09\nFull time\n\nPosition:   Staff Psychologist, Brandeis Counseling Center Location: Brandeis University – Waltham, MA Staff Psychologist - Wide-ranging clinical expertise and cultural responsiveness to support students from a variety of backgrounds, identities, and experiences. At Brandeis University, we believe mental health is foundational to student success. Our Brandeis Counseling Center (BCC) plays a vital role in nurturing the emotional well-being of a dynamic, richly varied student population, and passionate student body. We are looking for a collaborative and compassionate psychologist to join our team as Staff Psychologist — Someone who brings wide-ranging clinical expertise and cultural responsiveness to support students from a variety of backgrounds, identities, and experiences. The hiring range for this position is $61,100 - $117,500. (level and salary are commensurate with experience) What You will Do As a Staff Psychologist providing clinical care to students from a variety of backgrounds, identities, and experiences, you will: Support student wellness : Deliver short-term, solution-focused individual therapy, crisis intervention, and case management as needed. Foster learning : Conduct outreach programs and workshops to promote mental health awareness to students and staff. Collaborate across campus : Work closely with student-facing departments within the Student Affairs Division, as well as our campus partners at the Intercultural Center, Gender and Sexuality Center, Academic Services, Posse, Upward Bound, and other initiatives supporting students broadly to ensure holistic student support. Advance access : Support our mission to be a center that provides access to a wide range of students. Provide training and support: Offer training, technical assistance, consultation, and support to colleagues across campus and within the Brandeis Counseling Center. What You Bring Current licensure in the state of Massachusetts as a licensed psychologist or expectation that candidate is licensed-eligible within 12 months of hire. Has experience providing counseling services in an outpatient setting.  Demonstrated ability to provide culturally responsive care and work effectively with students from a broad range of backgrounds, identities, and communities. A warm, team-oriented approach and genuine enthusiasm for being visible and involved on campus.  Our team often presents at running groups, connecting with students at outreach events, and participating in mental health training for campus staff. Why Work at Brandeis? Be part of a mission-driven team that cares deeply about community, inclusivity, and student success. Join a supportive, collaborative environment where your ideas are valued and your expertise makes a real difference. Engage in meaningful professional development through working groups, trainings, and peer consultation. Enjoy fantastic benefits , including: Comprehensive health and wellness benefits Generous paid time off and holidays Free on-campus parking Free gym membership Ready to join a team that is reimagining campus mental health?   Apply today by submitting your cover letter and resume. Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Interim-Associate-Director--Undergraduate-Admissions--On-Campus---Virtual-Events_R0013768-1": {
      "title": "Interim Associate Director, Undergraduate Admissions- On-Campus & Virtual Events",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-09\nFull time\n\nBrandeis University Undergraduate Admissions is accepting applications for the temporary position of Interim Associate Director of Undergraduate Admissions, On-Campus and Virtual Events. The Interim Associate Director will plan and execute a strategy for all on-campus and virtual event programming such as daily visits, prospective student open houses, admitted student visit days, group visits and myriad online events. This position partners closely with colleagues across campus, including the Conference & Events Office, Student Life Office, faculty members, and Facility Services. Reporting to the Dean of Admissions, this position will serve as a direct supervisor for a team of up to five full-time professional staff members. In addition, this individual will serve as a member of the undergraduate admissions leadership team and support the overall execution of strategic departmental, division, and institutional enrollment goals.   The hiring range for this 6 month position is $40k - $41.8k This is a short term position with benefits. The anticipated employment is November 2026 - April 2027. Key Responsibilities: Serve as the lead event strategist and manager for all on-campus and virtual programming for both prospective and admitted students. Ensure an exceptional visit experience including training and coaching of admissions counseling staff.  Oversee the daily operations of the campus visit experience. Use data-driven decision making to evaluate the efficacy of this programming; propose and implement changes based on data. Manage an operational budget for all event programming. Supervise four-five professional staff members who are responsible for the management of a 40-person student fellows program, group visits, selection related projects, domestic recruitment, and other projects. Develop a comprehensive understanding of the holistic admissions process to effectively communicate with prospective students and families and thoughtfully inform campus programming. Schedule and communicate on-duty coverage, deliver admissions presentations and meet individually with prospective students and families as needed. Education & Requirements: Bachelor’s Degree required; Master’s Degree preferred. 5-8+ years related work experience, including supervisory/management experience.  What you need to succeed: Exceptional process management skills, organizational skills, problem solving, and decision making. Ability to strategically utilize and analyze data to make informed recommendations and decisions.  Must be organized, an excellent communicator and collaborator, with the ability to prioritize and work with multiple deadlines.  Ability to build strong relationships with admissions staff, high school counselors, and constituents across campus. Computer skills - proficiency with Microsoft applications required; Slate Technolutions, Workday preferred. A valid driver’s license is required. Evening and weekend work are necessary during key periods.         You must submit a cover letter with your application for consideration. You should upload the letter with your resume; there is not a separate prompt for a cover letter in the candidate application system.  The Admissions Office works in a hybrid fashion with exceptions for certain periods of the year. Typically, two scheduled remote days per week are planned after the first three months of employment. We have one day each week when all staff are scheduled to be on-campus. Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Part-time-Business-Coordinator_R0013760-1": {
      "title": "Part-time Business Coordinator",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-09\nPart time\n\nThe Business Coordinator for the School of Science, Engineering and Technology (SET) will perform a combination of responsibilities under the direction of the Business Administration Manager and demonstrate in-depth proficiency in completing routine assignments. Specific responsibilities vary by position and during the year, but include general office support, facilities support, and student and research administration support. Act as a knowledge resource for faculty, staff, and students. Position may work with their supervisor to hire and manage office students. Job Description General Operations (50%): Oversee general office support, including but not limited to covering the front desk; handling phone calls and foot traffic; scheduling rooms; handling key/card access; placing supply orders; handling mail/packages and checks; updating mail boxes; organizing office files and supplies; manage google calendars; maintaining contact lists; updating databases; and ensuring that the office is tidy and presentable. Facilities Management (20%): Serve as a contact for SET facilities concerns including placing and tracking work orders; conducting space and equipment surveys; organizing annual floor cleaning; and interacting with vendors and on-campus offices regarding school equipment purchasing, maintenance, and repairs particularly for autoclaves, ice machines, and glasswashers. Financial Responsibilities (10%): May be responsible for orders and other transactions processed in COUPA; assist faculty on P card reconciliations and transaction approvals; and process reimbursement and travel and business expense reports. Work in conjunction with procurement, general accounting, and accounts payable as necessary. Student and Research Staff Administration (15%): May be the primary contact for hourly student hiring and, in conjunction with Student Financial Services, coordinate hiring, costing allocation changes, termination, and other processes related to hourly students. May assist with processing of research staff, postdoctoral fellow, courtesy appointments, and stipended undergraduates in Workday. Other duties as assigned (5%): Other duties as required. Support cross-functional groups as requested and provide coverage for colleagues upon request. The hourly pay rate for the position is $25.82-$31. This is a part-time position working 17.5 hours a week and is eligible for benefits. Job Requirements: High School diploma required, Bachelor’s Degree preferred 1-3 years work-related experience is required. Strong organizational, communication, and interpersonal skills is essential. Attention to detail is an absolutely required quality. Must be able to work independently while having the ability to know when to ask questions. This position will be interacting with a wide range of faculty and staff to organize and handle logistics of incoming seminar speakers and scheduling of retreats. As such this position must be able to deftly adapt to any situation, concern, or problem that arises. This position is an outward facing position. Tasks performed will be highly visible to the community and external parties necessitating a highly professional and calm demeanor. Ability and inclination to anticipate future opportunities and consequences. Resonance with the mission and goals of the team’s priorities Experience and comfort with using with software is a must; preferably Filemaker, MS Office, Google suite, and institutional enterprise software. Expected to be a part of diverse teams, requiring high degree of collaboration and inspiring confidence with team members Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Senior-Research-Associate--Cohen-Center_R0013765-1": {
      "title": "Senior Research Associate, Cohen Center",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-09\nFull time\n\nThe Cohen Center for Modern Jewish Studies is looking for a Senior Research Associate I. The Sr. Research Associate's primary responsibility is to work closely with the PI to conduct all phases of complex research projects including proposal development, design, data collection, analysis, and interpretation of study results through reports, academic articles, and presentations. Manage field work, including conduct telephone and in-person interviews, focus groups, and site visits. Develop, administer, and analyze surveys. Author reports and pier-reviewed journal articles. Key Responsibilities: Serves as project manager on major research studies. Conceptualizes and oversees complex project research. Recommends research design to PIs/Co-PIs and other team members. Prepares overall project plans and budgets. Manages field work: from creation of instruments to the preparation of final manuscripts. Analyzes a wide variety of complex quantitative and qualitative data 65% Provides day-to-day direction to regular research staff and student workers on assigned projects. Mentors and train less experienced research staff. May have full managerial responsibility 15% Authors reports and pier-reviewed journal articles; presents and publishes findings. 15% Performs other assignments as needed. 5% The salary range for the position is $74,200-$92,700. Requirements: Doctoral degree (PhD) in a social science or humanities field with a research focus, such as psychology, sociology, or public policy preferred, or equivalent work experience. 1-3 years of relevant work experience is required Works on multiple projects across the center, so must be willing to juggle multiple responsibilities and work across teams. Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Temporary-Grant-Administrator--DISC-Post-Award_R0013577-1": {
      "title": "Temporary Grant Administrator, DISC Pre-Award",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-07\nFull time\n\nTemporary Grant Administrator, DISC Pre-Award  The Grant Administrator, DISC Pre-Award supports departmental and institutional efforts in securing external funding for research, training, and creative activities. Rooted in an understanding of federal regulations governing sponsored projects, other sponsor requirements, and institutional compliance requirements, the Grant Administrator serves as a primary liaison between principal investigators (PIs) and the institutional Pre-Award Services team. Under minimal oversight, they provide expert guidance and act as a trusted advisor to PIs throughout the proposal development process, ensuring all submissions comply with sponsor guidelines and institutional policies. The salary range for the position is $36-$40 per hour. Primary job duties: Supports a portfolio of sponsored projects proposals and ensures a high-quality product is delivered to the Pre-Award Services team for review and submission, including assisting in the development, preparation, finalization, and submission of the proposals. Obtains and reviews sponsor guidelines for all proposals; advises the PI on administrative requirements in preparing proposal submissions. Coordinates and reviews certain proposal elements (biosketches and facilities & resources) for consistency, accuracy, and completeness. Reviews proposals for consistency with sponsor guidelines on format, compliance with the FOA, font size and page limits, narrative, etc. Works closely with the PI to develop budgets and budget justification, ensuring accuracy. Reviews budgets for consistency with sponsor requirements; monitors for compliance with agency and University guidelines; verifies all financial information, including institutional salaries, fringe, indirect rates, etc. for the project.    Ensures all applications meet sponsor and institutional guidelines and deadlines, including management of the Just-In-Time process. Confers with PI and pre-award support staff at collaborating institutions to secure necessary sub-awards documents for proposals.  Monitors and facilitates the proposal routing process in compliance with University procedures.  All other duties, as assigned. This position is a hybrid position with an in-office requirement. Please submit a cover letter and resume. Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/PT-Athletic-Club-Sports-Coaches_R0013759-1": {
      "title": "PT Athletic Club Sports Coaches",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-06\nPart time\n\nResponsibilities of a Brandeis Club Sports Coach/Instructor may include the following: Conduct safe, organized, and productive practices and training sessions that help club members develop and improve their sport-specific skills. Provide sport-specific instruction, coaching, and constructive feedback appropriate to the team's experience and competitive level. Work collaboratively with student club leaders to establish practice plans, team goals, and expectations. Support the personal, athletic, and leadership development of student-athletes. Maintain a coaching philosophy consistent with the goals and student-development mission of the Brandeis Club Sports program and the Department of Athletics, Physical Education & Recreation. Promote and model good sportsmanship, fair play, respect, and positive team culture. Represent Brandeis University and the Club Sports program in a professional and positive manner at all times. Prioritize the safety and well-being of all participants during practices, competitions, and team activities. Travel with the team to competitions when required and provide appropriate coaching and supervision during team travel. Ensure that all participants follow applicable Brandeis University, Club Sports, facility, league, and governing-body policies and rules. Support student leaders in maintaining a team environment free from alcohol, illegal substances, harassment, discrimination, and other inappropriate conduct during Club Sports activities. Assist student leaders in ensuring that participants have completed all required Club Sports registration, waiver, and eligibility requirements before participating in practices or competitions. Communicate regularly with student club leaders and/or the Club Sports Administrator regarding team activities, scheduling, competitions, and concerns that may arise. Take part in other team or Club Sports responsibilities as appropriate to the needs of the program. Hiring Range: $1500 - $8000. Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Operations-Coordinator--Brandeis-Career-Network_R0013757-1": {
      "title": "Operations Coordinator, Brandeis Career Network",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-06\nFull time\n\nBrandeis University is launching a bold new approach to career as part of its vision to Reinvent the Liberal Arts , and we’re looking for an enthusiastic, highly organized Operations Coordinator to help bring that vision to life. The Operations Coordinator is at the center of the day-to-day experience of the Brandeis Career Network. We’re looking for someone who takes pride in creating a welcoming, professional environment, enjoys helping people, notices the details others might miss, and gets satisfaction from making sure everything runs smoothly. You’ll help create an exceptional experience for the students, alumni, faculty, staff, employers, and other visitors who interact with our team. Key Responsibilities Office Management Serve as a primary point of contact for students, alumni, staff, and visitors, providing responsive customer service in person, by phone, and by email. Oversee front desk and student-facing operations, including scheduling appointments, answering questions, sharing resources, and making referrals. Maintain a clean, organized, and welcoming office environment. Hire, train, schedule, and supervise student employees supporting office operations. Manage office correspondence, supplies, equipment, inventory, and confidential materials. Process purchasing, reimbursements, invoices, and other budget transactions. Identify opportunities to improve office processes, systems, and the overall experience of staff and visitors. Team Support Provide administrative and operational support to a growing, collaborative career center team. Maintain accurate student and alumni records, including appointments, notes, and follow-up documentation. Enter and manage job postings, resume collections, events, and other information in Handshake and related systems. Support career programs and events through room reservations, catering, registration, materials, communications, and other event logistics. Coordinate with university offices and campus partners to help the team navigate processes and connect students with appropriate resources. Assist with special projects and emerging priorities as the Brandeis Career Network continues to grow and evolve. Executive and Administrative Support Provide administrative support to the Executive Director, Career Services, Education and Strategy. Assist with calendar management and scheduling, including prioritizing requests and helping resolve scheduling conflicts. Coordinate meetings and events, including agendas, materials, room reservations, technology, and follow-up. Take meeting notes and prepare clear summaries, action items, and follow-up materials as needed. Draft, edit, and format correspondence, reports, presentations, and other materials. Track projects, deadlines, and follow-up items to help ensure work moves forward. Support a positive and connected team culture by coordinating staff meetings, celebrations, recognition, and other team activities. Why You’ll Love Working Here Be part of something new. Join a growing career organization during an exciting period of transformation at Brandeis. Make a visible impact. Help create a welcoming, well-run environment for students, visitors, and colleagues. Enjoy variety in your work. Support everything from office operations and events to student employees and special projects. Grow with us. Brandeis supports professional development, training, and opportunities to build new skills. Work Environment Brandeis combines the educational model of a liberal arts college with the resources of a research university. At our core, we are a medium-sized residential campus that provides a robust in-person experience for students and community members. Career center staff work 35 hours per week on a hybrid schedule, with flexible hours available when operational needs allow. Occasional early morning, evening, or weekend work may be required to support programs and events. Benefits Brandeis offers a comprehensive and competitive benefits package designed to support employees' health, financial well-being, and work-life balance. Benefits include medical, dental, and vision coverage, disability and life insurance, retirement savings, tuition assistance, employee discounts, and voluntary insurance options. Hiring Salary Range: $52,000–$58,000 Qualifications Required High school diploma or GED. One to three years of administrative, office operations, customer service, or related experience. Demonstrated ability to provide high-quality customer service with professionalism, discretion, and confidentiality. Strong organizational skills, attention to detail, and the ability to manage multiple priorities. Ability to identify problems, develop solutions, and follow projects through to completion. Strong written and verbal communication skills for a variety of audiences. Proficiency with Microsoft Office, Google Workspace, and web-based systems and platforms. Experience tracking expenses, processing transactions, or supporting budget management. Ability to represent the Brandeis Career Network professionally in person, online, by phone, and through written communication. Ability to adapt and stay organized in a collaborative, fast-moving, and evolving work environment. Preferred Bachelor’s degree. Administrative or operational experience in a college, university, nonprofit, or similarly complex organization. Experience supervising student employees or other part-time staff. Familiarity with platforms such as Handshake, LinkedIn, or other student recruiting, CRM, or event-management systems. Apply If you’re highly organized, enjoy helping people, and like being the person who makes things work, we’d love to hear from you. Please submit a resume and cover letter describing your interest in the position and the experience you would bring to the Brandeis Career Network. Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis. Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Police-Officer-I_R0013743-2": {
      "title": "Police Officer I",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-05\nFull time\n\nUnder the direct supervision of the Assistant Director of Public Safety and the Duty Shift Sergeant, the University Police Officer I will patrol University buildings, grounds, and adjacent areas and enforce the Commonwealth of Massachusetts laws and University regulations therein. Project an image of willing and helpful community service in the interest of campus security, crime prevention, and the overall well being of the general community. University Police Officers are expected to follow instructions of Public Safety Daily Reports and must be thoroughly familiar with the Brandeis University Campus Police Department Manual. The hourly rate for this position is $35.37, 40 hour work week. Key Responsibilities: Patrol University buildings, grounds and adjacent areas at regular intervals. Protect the campus from trespassers, thefts, damage or injury to persons and property. Inspect interior and exterior of all University buildings; extinguish unnecessary light; secure exterior door; lock offices, laboratories and related rooms found unlocked, unless otherwise requested. Report all irregularities to the appropriate authorities. Respond immediately to all intrusion/fire alarms; investigate causes for same and perform necessary related follow-up responsibilities dictated by circumstances at scene and as outlined in written fire alarm response procedures. 40% Exercise police powers in the detection and apprehension of law violators in a judicious manner, exercising discretion and good judgment at all times; investigate all complaints and refer to the appropriate supervisory personnel as deemed necessary. Prepare detailed, clearly written investigative reports of all incidents taking place during tour of duty. 20% Enforce University vehicle parking and traffic regulations. Investigate vehicular accidents and provide required documentation. Control vehicular and pedestrian traffic entering/exiting campus, so as to promote maximum safety. Exercise control over limited parking facilities and exclude unauthorized and undesirable parties from campus properties. 15% May perform overtime campus police detail duties as requested during University social/athletic functions.  May provide emergency and/or courtesy transportation for students, faculty and staff. 15% When assigned to dispatch duty, acknowledge all incoming personal visit/telephone reports at the communication center. Handle all inquiries and/or dispatch patrol cars for action. Control UHF (two-way) communications network at the communications center repeater console, linking the Public Safety office and the patrol cruisers. Monitor alarms and CCTV and dispatch patrol cars for appropriate action. 10% Job Requirements: Associates degree required plus up to 2 years of related work experience. MA state driver’s license required. At least 21 years of age Required: Successful completion of the Commonwealth P.O.S.T certified academy . Must be able to obtain and retain a Massachusetts Class A license to carry a firearm and successfully complete firearms qualifications per Department standards. First Aid and CPR certification. Successful completion of medical/physical as required by the University, inclusive of drug and alcohol testing with no restrictions. Successful completion of the pre-employment psychological examination, as determined by a qualified independent psychologist. Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Senior-Customer-Service---Systems-Administrator_R0013630-1": {
      "title": "Senior Customer Service & Systems Administrator",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-05\nFull time\n\nSenior Customer Service & Systems Administrator | Brandeis University | Waltham, MA Hiring Range: $63.9k - $95.2k The anticipated hiring salary is generally expected to fall near the midpoint of the range, based on the candidate’s qualifications, skills, experience, and internal equity. Offers above the midpoint may be considered for candidates with exceptional qualifications and directly relevant experience.  This is an in-person position. The work schedule is Monday - Friday, 7AM - 3PM. Please submit a resume and cover letter to be considered for this position. Exciting opportunity available supporting the Executive Director, of Operations, Administration, and Budget. About the Role The Senior Customer Services and Systems Administrator plays a key administrative and operational support role within the Campus Planning and Operations group, providing administrative support for facility management technology and systems used across Campus Operations. This person will support the identification and implementation of technology and process improvements that improve operational efficiency, staff productivity, and work quality. The position has primary administrative responsibility for the Facilities Maximo computerized maintenance management system (CMMS). This role also maintains Campus Operations website updates for Campus Operations. The position works with Communications and appropriate Campus Operations departments to ensure website information is accurate, current, accessible, and user-friendly. This role also supports the day-to-day administration of Facilities Customer Service and Work Management operations and serves as an initial point of contact for the campus experiences with the Facilities organization. This includes intake of all service requests from the University Community, supporting the processing and assignment of work orders for the facilities services team as well as resolution of all customer service issues. This position also provides technical support and system administration for the University’s AIMS Parking Management Software, including configuration, troubleshooting, data management, and user access. Maintains system functionality and data accuracy and assists with resolving technical issues and supporting integrations related to campus parking operations. This individual is also responsible for supporting a positive end user experience for our community and continuous review and improvement of the service. This individual will also provide monthly billing of custodial, technical trades and fuel services, as well as monthly or weekly reporting on open or closed work orders and KPI data to key personnel across the campus community, University Events, Finance and Facilities leadership. The position works closely with the Supervisor of Work Order Operations to support the effective administration, tracking, reporting, and resolution of Facilities work orders and customer service requests. Key Responsibilities: Customer Service Reporting to the Exec Director of Operations, Administration and Budget, this position provides administrative support to the Facilities Work Management group, coordinating the daily intake and processing of work orders. Supports the resolution of all customer service issues, troubleshooting and working through to resolution. Interacts with departmental field staff, supervisors, managers and other senior staff, as well as a broad range of customers and constituents both within the University and external to Brandeis. Provides regular reporting from the Maximo workorder system for monthly financial chargebacks, open service tickets to departments and KPI data. Works closely with the Supervisor of Work Order Operations to monitor work order activity, identify issues requiring follow-up, and support timely processing and closure of service requests. Supports communication with departments regarding work order status, service requests, and customer service issues. Material’s Management and Stockroom Work with the Associate Director, Facilities Support Services, who will oversee the vendor, to provide administrative support for facilities materials management and the outsourced stockroom operation. Monitor inventory levels and supply usage to help ensure appropriate stock levels are maintained and operational needs are met. Coordinate with the outsourced stockroom provider and Facilities staff regarding inventory, supply requirements, deliveries, and discrepancies. Assist with periodic inventory counts and review inventory data to identify trends, shortages, excess inventory, or opportunities for improved inventory management. Support the accurate allocation and chargeback of materials to the appropriate Facilities organization.  Processes and Technology Supports and participates in technical and process related projects primarily within Facilities Services, as well as across the Campus Planning and Operations & Public Safety organizations that includes Campus Planning, Environmental Health and Safety, Capital Programs, and Public Safety. Identifies opportunities to improve processes, efficiency, and the use of technology and makes recommendations to appropriate leadership. Works closely with ITS on the annual computer refreshes for Campus Planning and Operations. Responsible for the creation and tracking of key performance indicators (KPls) for facilities. Creates and provides a wide variety of reports for measuring process and operational efficiencies along with other reports as needed for general data requests relevant to Maximo. Analyzes data and makes recommendations to departmental staff. Responsible for the development and maintenance of the Campus Operations website. This includes ensuring information is kept updated and review happens on a regular timeframe. Also ensures the site is modern looking, current and user friendly. Works closely with the Communications department to ensure accessibility and communication standards are met as well as photography. Provides technical oversight daily for Maximo and related DataSplice mobility platform, including user set up and maintenance, set up and configurations of start center portlets, and screen modifications. Troubleshoots and resolves standard technical and functional issues within Maximo and DataSplice and/or other related applications, systems, and devices. Provides additional technical support and assistance to Facilities staff for common issues with desktops, laptops, tablets, phones, radios, scanners, etc. Supports the computer refresh in coordination with the IT department. The role includes analyzing and documenting various business processes across the entire Campus Planning and Operations organization. Provides periodic training on the use of Facilities systems and databases for existing staff as well as with onboarding for new employees. This includes creation and updating of training materials. Works to improve end user interactions with systems. Administrative and Student Support Provides administrative support and coordination for the Facilities Work Management function. Works closely with the Supervisor of Work Order Operations to support daily work management activities and effective customer service operations. Coordinates the daily activities and assignments of Facilities student employees, as assigned. Assists with onboarding, training, scheduling, and administrative support for student employees. Maintains administrative records and documentation related to assigned responsibilities. Provides support for special projects and process improvement initiatives as assigned. Education: B.A./B.S. or equivalent required Experience: 3-5-years required, preferably more and preferably in a higher education environment. The ideal candidate will have working knowledge and experience with Maximo computerized maintenance management system (CMMS).  Experience with AIMS Parking Management Software is also preferred. It is also preferred that the individual has some experience with Data Splice, and 25Live. Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Lecturer-in-English--Poetry-_R0013756": {
      "title": "Lecturer in English (Poetry)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-05\nPart time\n\nThe Department of English/Creative Writing at Brandeis University seeks an instructor for the course ENG 119b—Creative Writing Workshop: Poetry, a 4-credit course offered spring semester, 2027. This course is a poetry workshop limited to twelve students, undergraduates from first-year to seniors with varying levels of experience. The class meets for three hours once a week for a total of thirteen meetings. Candidates with an MFA in Creative Writing, at least two years of teaching experience, and publication of their poetry in significant literary journals will be given preference. Interested individuals should submit a cover letter, CV, and names and contact information for two references. The deadline for first consideration is October 9, 2026. This appointment is to a position that is in a collective bargaining unit represented by SEIU Local 509.  The compensation for this course is $8764.20. Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Temporary-Corporate---Workforce-Partnerships-Associate_R0013754-2": {
      "title": "Temporary Corporate & Workforce Partnerships Associate",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-05\nPart time\n\nBrandeis University is delighted to announce a Temporary Corporate & Workforce Partnership Associate - Part-time position in the Rabb Online School. This is a hybrid position; Wednesdays in the office. This is a part-time position, 20 hours per week. Anticipated length of assignment- 3 months. The hourly rate for this position is $44.00 per hour.   Key Responsibilities: Relationship Management (65%): Host meetings and maintain regular communication with current corporate partners to maintain and strengthen relationships. This includes a standing check-in cadence with each partner's HR, benefits, or learning contact, sharing program updates and upcoming enrollment deadlines, and keeping the relationship active between events. Engage with partners through email correspondence and phone calls to provide updates and gather feedback. Respond to partner questions about the tuition benefit, eligibility, and application steps, and bring partner feedback back to Admissions and Marketing. Host webinars and information sessions to showcase Brandeis Online programs and explore opportunities for collaboration. Schedule sessions with the partner contact, coordinate the presenter and registration with Admissions, run the session, and follow up with attendees. Coordinate and staff select partner benefits fairs to enhance partner engagement and satisfaction. Prioritize fairs at partners with the largest employee populations, staff the table, speak with employees about programs and the partner discount, and capture leads for Admissions follow-up. Onboard newly signed partners. Walk the partner through how the benefit works, provide the landing page, flyers, and internal announcement copy, and get the first employee information session on the calendar.   Data Management (20%): Maintain a corporate dashboard to track partner engagement, interactions, and key metrics. Log partner contacts, meetings, events, and next actions so the status of every partnership is current and visible to leadership. Utilize internal CRM systems to manage partner information and generate reports for analysis. Use Slate and the partnership dashboards to keep partner records current, confirm partner tagging with Admissions, and pull enrollment by partner after each intake.   Communications and collaboration (15%): Draft and distribute newsletters and other relevant communications to partners, students, and stakeholders. Prepare regular partner updates covering program news, upcoming sessions, and deadlines, and adapt the content for partner employee newsletters and intranet posts. Ensure timely and informative communication to keep partners informed and engaged. Respond to partner requests promptly, confirm event details, and keep partners aware of what is coming next. Work closely with various departments within Brandeis, including marketing, the registrar, IT, and student services, to ensure seamless coordination in achieving admissions and enrollment goals. Coordinate with Marketing on partner flyers, landing pages, and newsletter content, and with Admissions and Student Services on partner student questions. Other duties as assigned.   Education & Work Experience: Bachelor's degree in business, marketing, communications, or a related field (Master's degree preferred). 3-5 years of experience in partnership development, account management, or business development, preferably in higher education or online learning.   Job Requirements: Strong interpersonal and communication skills, with the ability to build rapport and negotiate effectively with diverse stakeholders. Excellent organizational skills and attention to detail, with the ability to manage multiple projects simultaneously. Ability to work independently and step into established partner relationships quickly. Experience hosting virtual information sessions or webinars. Proficiency in Slate and Microsoft Office Suite. Willingness to travel locally for partner meetings and events. Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Career-Counselor---Business--Consulting--and-Finance_R0013448-1": {
      "title": "Career Counselor - Business, Consulting, and Finance",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-02\nFull time\n\nHelp students discover what's possible. Build a career center that's redefining careers. Brandeis University is launching a bold new approach to career development as part of its vision to Reinvent the Liberal Arts. We're creating an integrated career center that serves undergraduate students, graduate students, and alumni by connecting academic learning with career exploration, internships, employer partnerships, and lifelong career support. This is an exciting opportunity to join a growing team and help shape the future of career education at one of the nation's leading liberal arts research universities. We're expanding our undergraduate career team and hiring a Career Counselor for our Business, Consulting, and Finance industry hub. All counselors work with students across a wide range of majors and career interests while also developing an area of emphasis within one of our industry hubs; this opportunity in particular supports the Business, Consulting & Finance hub, including corporate careers, business strategy, consulting, finance, accounting, and marketing.  Experience or an academic background in one of these areas is helpful but not required. We provide extensive onboarding, mentoring, and professional development to help every counselor build expertise. Key Responsibilities: Career Development Provide career counseling to undergraduate students and alumni through every stage of the career development process, while supporting graduate students as needed. Help students explore career options, make informed decisions, identify strengths, set goals, conduct career and graduate school research, and navigate internship and job searches. Apply career development theory and best practices to meet individual student needs. Teach career courses by developing course materials, facilitating engaging classroom instruction, and assessing student learning. Maintain knowledge in career resources, and employment trends, while staying current on labor market data, recruiting practices, and emerging career opportunities. Campus Engagement Build strong partnerships with faculty, staff, and campus partners to advance the mission of the Career Center. Collaborate across the university to develop innovative programs, workshops, and events that integrate career development into the student experience. Create engaging career resources, presentations, student communications, and online content that supports career readiness and encourages student engagement. Preferred Qualifications: A Bachelor’s degree with 3 years of work experience, or a Master’s degree with up to 1 year of work experience, is required. Prior experience working with youth or young adults, teaching, coaching, or customer service is preferred. Experience working with a broad range of student populations is preferred. Exceptional written and verbal communication skills, along with strong interpersonal abilities, are required.   Why You'll Love Working Here Join a growing, forward-thinking career center during an exciting period of transformation Teach, coach, and mentor students while building innovative career education programs Collaborate with faculty, alumni, and employers across the university Receive support for professional development, conference attendance, certifications, and leadership opportunities Work on a collaborative team that values creativity, continuous learning, and student success Work Environment: Brandeis combines the educational model of a liberal arts college with the resources of a research university. At our core, we are a medium-sized residential campus that provides a robust in-person experience for students and community members. Career staff work 35 hours per week on a hybrid schedule, with flexible hours when available. Occasional early morning, evening, or weekend work may be required. Benefits: We invest in our staff. Career counselors receive ongoing training, mentorship, support for professional certifications, conference attendance, and opportunities to become leaders in the career services profession. Brandeis also offers a comprehensive and competitive benefits package designed to support your health, financial well-being, and work-life balance. Benefits include medical, dental, vision, disability, and life insurance; retirement savings; tuition assistance; employee discounts; voluntary insurance options; and free campus parking . Hiring Salary Ranges: $62,800 - $67,900 Apply:  If you're passionate about helping students discover possibilities, build confidence, and achieve their goals, we'd love to hear from you. Learn more about the undergraduate career team .  Submit your resume and cover letter, and indicate which Industry Hub you would like to be considered for. Join us in reshaping the future of career development for Brandeis students and alumni as a member of the new Brandeis Career Center.  We will be reviewing applications on a rolling basis.   Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Lecturer-in-Theater-Arts--Lighting-Design-_R0013736": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Theater Arts (Lighting Design)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-01\nPart time\n\nThe Department of Theater Arts at Brandeis University seeks an instructor for THA 135b Lighting Design for Live Performance , a four-credit course in the spring 2027 semester. Candidates with experience teaching Lighting Design will be given preference. Interested individuals should submit a cover letter, CV, a draft course syllabus, and names and contact information for two references. This appointment is to a position that is in a collective bargaining unit represented by SEIU Local 509.  The compensation for this course is $8764.20. Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Temporary-Community-Educator---Fall-2026_R0013733-1": {
      "title": "Temporary Community Educator - Fall 2026",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-30\nFull time\n\nBetter to Learn(B2L) is a partnership between Brandeis University and Legacy Heritage Fund that brings together Jewish teens in weekly online learning sessions. In addition, students are invited to the Boston area for a four-day residency in late December to engage in thoughtful learning, hear guest speakers, connect with their online peers and visit Brandeis University. We seek Community Educators to support students and programming at the B2L December Retreat. Responsibilities Attend the December 20-23 Retreat and stay in the hotel ( Hilton Garden Inn, Waltham ) with students. Supervise B2L students at the hotel, on the program bus and on campus during all activities, including in the evening after programming has ended (ie: make sure students are in their rooms, at breakfast and on the bus in the morning; handle any student concerns or disciplinary issues; address minor medical issues–with support from the Residential Coordinator). In collaboration with other Community Educators, run 7 programs (already created-–timing ranges from 30 minutes to 1 hour 45 mins) connected to the program theme. Provide administrative support to B2L Directors, Instructors and Guest Speakers as requested (ie: get supplies and materials, make copies, set up and break down program spaces, including meals). Act as a positive Jewish role model for B2L participants. Qualifications Current undergraduate, recent college graduate or graduate student. Experience as a camp counselor, youth worker or religious school teacher. Experience working with and supervising teens. Creative, flexible and enthusiastic. Either does not have a Brandeis student job, or has a job that ends by December 16, 2026. Compensation : $800 plus room and board (kosher catering for all meals provided by Dushez Catering ). Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Temporary-Instructor---Better-to-Learn_R0013731-1": {
      "title": "Temporary Instructor - Better to Learn",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-30\nFull time\n\nBetter to Learn is a partnership between Brandeis University Precollege Programs and Legacy Heritage Fund that brings together Jewish teens in weekly online learning sessions. In addition, students will be invited to the Boston area for a three-day residency in late December for thoughtful in-person learning with our instructors and  guest speakers. They will also  connect with their online peers and tour Brandeis University.   Brandeis University seeks Instructors to teach the Better to Learn courses. Instructors will be employed by Brandeis University.   Responsibilities Create a 10-session semester-long course on a topic approved in advance by Brandeis and LHF on the following dates (last 2 sessions to be taught in person at the Seminar); October 18 (Orientation at 5:30PM), 25 November 1, 8, 15, 22 December 6, 13 Provide course syllabus to Brandeis and LHF staff at least one month prior to the beginning of classes. Prepare suggested readings for student (1 per session). Deliver 1 hour course sessions via Zoom (either from 6-7pm EST or 7:15-8:15pm EST) on Sunday evenings. Register for a Brandeis email address and host Zoom session (tech set up by Brandeis). Read and evaluate students’ final papers.   Qualifications Advanced academic degree or coursework in Judaic Studies. Experience in developing and implementing curriculum for teens. Experience teaching and working with teens. Must be creative, flexible and able to comply with all program policies.   Compensation : One Class per semester + Winter Retreat: $6,825 Two classes per semester + Winter Retreat: $13650   Please apply through the Brandeis Career Page Workday portal. And include a resume and cover letter. Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Temporary-Residential-Coordinator---Fall-2026_R0013732-1": {
      "title": "Temporary Residential Coordinator - Fall 2026",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-30\nFull time\n\nBetter to Learn is a partnership between Brandeis University and Legacy Heritage Fund that brings together Jewish teens in weekly online learning sessions. In addition, students are invited to the Boston area for a three day residency in late December to engage in thoughtful learning, hear guest speakers, connect with their online peers and visit Brandeis University. We seek a Residential Coordinator to supervise staff and support students and programming at the B2L December Retreat. Responsibilities Attend the December 20-23 Retreat and stay in the hotel ( Hilton Garden Inn, Waltham ) with students and Community Educators; Supervise B2L Community Educators and provide any guidance and support needed; Oversee all Retreat programming (pre-planned); Oversee all Retreat logistics and programmatic needs; Serve as the point person for Instructors and Guest Speakers during the Retreat; In consultation with the B2L Project Director, plan and attend B2L Retreat Orientation (via Zoom); Meet with the Project Director 2-4 times before the Retreat to plan/organize; Assist with bringing supplies to the hotel and returning them to Brandeis; Act as a positive Jewish role model for B2L participants. Qualifications Undergraduate degree; Experience as a camp counselor, youth worker or religious school teacher; Experience working with and supervising teens; Must be creative, flexible and able to comply with all program policies. Compensation : $2000 plus room and board Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Senior-Department-Coordinator--Counseling---Health-Centers-_R0013624": {
      "title": "Senior Department Coordinator (Counseling & Health Centers)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-30\nFull time\n\nAre you a proactive administrative professional who thrives in a dynamic, high-impact environment? Do you excel at multi-tasking, managing communications, and serving as the operational heartbeat of a dedicated team? Our University’s Counseling and Health Centers are looking for an energetic, detail-oriented Senior Department Coordinator to support both vital departments. In this dual-impact role, you will be the primary point of contact for students, patients, staff, and campus leadership, ensuring our day-to-day operations, communications, and clinical administrative workflows run smoothly. Why This Role Matters The Counseling and Health Centers are essential to student wellness, health, and academic success. As the Senior Department Coordinator, your work directly supports our clinical teams and front-office staff, ensuring high-quality, compassionate care for our campus community. Reporting jointly to the Directors of both centers, you will balance operational excellence with creative outreach and data analysis. What You Will Do Operations & Workflow Management (60%) Front-Office & Clinical Support: Serve as the go-to resource for front-desk staff, provide front-office coverage, and ensure clinical schedules are accurately populated and maintained within the Electronic Health Record (EHR) system. Executive Administrative Support: Manage executive calendars for both Center Directors with minimal supervision, order department supplies, and maintain day-to-day office efficiency. Event & Logistics Coordination: Organize, schedule, and execute complex, high-impact meetings, campus initiatives, and departmental events from concept to logistics. Communications & Campus Outreach (25%) Digital Media & Web: Maintain website content to keep information accurate and up to date, while managing vibrant social media channels, newsletters, and email communications. Community Partner Engagement: Serve as a primary point of contact for campus and community outreach requests (including the BCC team), assisting with event planning and attending outreach functions. Collaborative Relationship Building: Act as a warm, welcoming, and knowledgeable front-line resource for visitors, students, and campus partners, fostering strong cross-departmental relationships. Reporting & Financial Data (10%) Data & Analytics: Compile, track, and maintain revenue and charge data reports to support financial monitoring and operational planning. Executive Presentations: Create clear, polished presentations and operational reports using Microsoft Excel, PowerPoint, and Office suite tools for department leadership. Special Projects & Other Duties (5%) Assist leadership with special projects, departmental priorities, and emerging initiatives as needed. What We Are Looking For Experience: Strong administrative, operational, or coordinator experience—preferably in higher education, healthcare, or clinical office settings. Technical Skills: Proficiency with Microsoft Office (especially Excel and PowerPoint), web content management systems, and social media platforms. Experience with Electronic Health Record (EHR) systems is a major plus. Communication & Discretion: Exceptional written and verbal communication skills, paired with sound judgment and a deep respect for student/patient privacy and confidentiality (HIPAA/FERPA awareness). Multi-Tasking Excellence: Ability to balance competing priorities across two distinct departments with autonomy, organization, and calm under pressure. Customer-Centric Mindset: A welcoming, empathetic demeanor toward students, clients, patients, and visitors navigating health and wellness services. The hiring range for this position is $24.45/hour - $28/hour How to Apply Ready to bring your administrative expertise to a supportive, student-focused team? Apply today with your resume and a brief cover letter outlining your experience in office operations, communications, or healthcare/counseling administrative support. Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Lecturer-in-Politics--Political-Theory-_R0013724": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Politics (Political Theory)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-28\nPart time\n\nThe Department of Politics at Brandeis University seeks an instructor for a 4-credit course in the subfield of political theory to be offered in Spring 2027. This course can be on a topic proposed by applicants. Possible topics are feminist theory, human rights, global justice, but others will be seriously considered. This course can be offered as an intermediate or advanced undergraduate course in political theory.  Candidates with an MA or PhD in political science and training in Political Theory will be given preference. Interested individuals should submit a cover letter, CV, draft course syllabi, and names and contact information for two references. The deadline for first consideration is October 15, 2026. This appointment is to a position that is in a collective bargaining unit represented by SEIU Local 509.  The compensation for this four-credit course is $8764.20. Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Lecturer-in-Artificial-Intelligence--AI----Management_R0013712": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Artificial Intelligence (AI) & Management",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-24\nPart time\n\nThe School of Business and Economics (SBE) at Brandeis University seeks an instructor for the AI for Management, a 4-credit course in Spring 2027 semester . This required MBA course prepares students first to lead the responsible adoption of AI, second to use AI effectively in their own work, and third to participate intelligently in the design of AI-enabled products and processes. The course provides a durable managerial foundation in how contemporary AI systems are trained and used, why they can produce fluent but wrong or unsafe results, and how evaluation and process controls reduce those risks. Students then apply that foundation to source-grounded research, data analysis, multimodal communication, specification-driven application building, workflow redesign, and enterprise implementation. Agentic AI is introduced early and examined in depth at a managerial level so that students can distinguish agents from fixed workflows and understand interactive, event-triggered, scheduled, proactive, tool-using, computer-using, transactional, single-agent, and multi-agent systems. The class meets for 1.5-hours, twice a week. Candidates with minimum qualification of doctoral (Ph.D.) degree along with relevant professional qualifications and experience with Agentic AI and no-code AI, machine learning and data science will be given preference. Interested individuals should submit a short cover letter, CV and names and contact information for two references. The deadline for first consideration is September 25, 2026 . Compensation for this position between $10,00 to $12,000 depending on qualifications. This appointment is to a position that is in a collective bargaining unit represented by SEIU Local 509.  Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Lecturer-in-Leadership-in-Sports-Business-Fundamentals_R0013711": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Leadership in Sports Business Fundamentals",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-24\nPart time\n\nThe School of Business and Economics (SBE) at Brandeis University seeks an instructor for a course in Sports Business Fundamentals for the spring 2027 semester. This course is part of the undergraduate curriculum (Sports Analytics - SPRT and specialized master’s program concentration) and may include topics such as: Survey of the sports industry at professional and/or collegiate team and league levels Detail budgeting and finance considerations specific to sports entities Understand venue, scheduling and team operations and processes Illustrate human resources required to successfully manage and operate the business Feature partnerships and government regulations Courses are designed to provide students with the foundational knowledge of sports as a business.  Subsequent courses will build on these fundamentals to utilize sports analytics to create a data-driven decision framework for analyzing sports business use cases. This particular course can be taught using case-based and/or experiential learning methods (including case discussions, simulations, and applied projects), and emphasize active student participation. Course assignments and scheduling will depend on program needs and instructor expertise. Candidates should have a minimum qualification of a Master’s degree (PhD preferred) in strategy, sports management, finance, or a related field, along with relevant professional and/or teaching experience. We particularly welcome applicants with industry experience in sports, consulting, technology, innovation management, corporate strategy, entrepreneurship, or related fields. Experience teaching using case-based or experiential methodologies is strongly preferred. Interested individuals should submit a cover letter, CV, course syllabi, a brief teaching statement, and names and contact information for two references. The deadline for first consideration is September 30, 2026. Compensation for this position is between $9,00 to $10,000 depending on qualifications. This appointment is to a position that is in a collective bargaining unit represented by SEIU Local 509. Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Lecturer-in-Leadership-in-Social-Innovation_R0013710": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Leadership in Social Innovation",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-24\nPart time\n\nThe School of Business and Economics (SBE) at Brandeis University seeks a co-instructor for the BUS 295a: Leadership in Social Impact Innovation, a 4-credit course in Spring 2027 semester. The class is an experiential course in which students work in small teams to solve real organizational challenges for social impact organizations, in the livelihoods, environmental, social, and governance space. Partner organizations include both not-for-profit and for-profit enterprises, ranging from small and local to multi-regional and global. Each team is matched with a partner organization and a mentor who helps define the scope of the engagement — a challenge with genuine strategic impact and tactical feasibility. Teams diagnose the problem, conduct research, develop recommendations, and deliver an implementation-ready product to their client by the end of the semester. Candidates with minimum qualification of doctoral (Ph.D.) degree along with relevant entrepreneurial qualifications and experience in Social Impact organizations will be given preference. Interested individuals should submit a short cover letter, CV and names and contact information for two references. The deadline for first consideration is September 25, 2026. Compensation for this position as a co-instructor will be in between $4,500 to $5,000 range depending on qualifications. This appointment is to a position that is in a collective bargaining unit represented by SEIU Local 509. Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Equipment-Mechanic-I--Lead_R0013146": {
      "title": "Equipment Mechanic I, Lead",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-23\nFull time\n\nBrandeis University is delighted to announce a career opportunity as the Equipment Mechanic I, Lead in the Facilities Department. At Brandeis, we offer a competitive benefits and compensation package which includes medical, dental and life insurances.  If you are looking to advance your career through educational opportunities, Brandeis offers tuition assistance for employees, dependents and spouses.  Our 403b retirement savings plan includes a generous match. Under the direction of the Manager, Grounds & Fleet, the Equipment Mechanic I, Lead, maintains, troubleshoots, diagnoses and repairs all types of vehicles and equipment owned and leased by Brandeis University. Manages the Facilities Services Fleet inspection program. Must have an extensive knowledge of diagnosing, repairing and maintaining all small engines as well as gas and diesel vehicles and equipment. This position is a Local 615, 32BJ Service Employees International Union (SEIU) position. The hourly rate for this position is $43.47, 40 hours per week Job Functions: Lead Equipment Mechanic Functions: Must have sufficient knowledge of small engines, gas and diesel engines, hydraulics and electrical systems. Relay information and assignments to others as directed by manager. Instruct and direct fellow employees assigned to assist Lead equipment mechanic Schedule routine and unexpected maintenance on all Brandeis owned/leased vehicles and equipment Schedules and ensures required Brandeis and State inspections are up to date Maintain parts and supply inventory for maintenance of Brandeis owned and leased vehicles and equipment. Assess and update all work order assignments using the department's computerized work order management system. This includes keeping records, writing reports, and identifying/requesting materials, and recording time in a timely manner. Make recommendations pertaining to the condition and longevity of all University owned/leased vehicles and equipment Main Functions: Diagnose nature and extent of equipment and vehicle malfunction. Raise vehicles and equipment utilizing hydraulic and floor jacks to gain access Replace, adjust, install and repair accessories (radios, heaters, mirrors, windshield wipers, headlights etc.) Utilize Equipment requiring a MA hoisting license Lubricate equipment and change engine and hydraulic oil and transmission fluids. Repair and install tires using a tire changing machine Corrects vehicle deficiencies by removing, repairing, adjusting, overhauling, assembling, disassembling, and replacing major assemblies, sub-assemblies, components, parts, or systems, such as, power and drive trains, electrical, air conditioning, fuel, emission, brake, steering, and hydraulics Utilize trucks and utility vehicles with plows and sanders, as well as front end loaders to remove and treat snow and ice from all areas on campus. Maintains and repairs small engine equipment including blowers, string trimmers and lawn mowers including blade sharpening Assist the Groundspersons team as directed by the manager. General Functions: Follow proper safety procedures and observe all safety rules and regulations including OSHA Respond to emergency call-ins as needed Read, write, understand, and carry out oral and written instructions in English. Participates in training to keep and acquire knowledge, skills, and abilities in order to remain current with standard operational practices and revised standards Establish and maintain a positive cooperative working relationship with supervisors, fellow workers, department heads, and other members of the campus community. Work in an environment subject to frequent changing climate conditions, priorities and deadlines. Drive a University Vehicle requiring Class B Commercial Driver’s License *This list is representative of those duties and responsibilities that are required of this position. However, the list is not to be considered as all-inclusive. Education, Licenses and Work Experience: Education: High School Diploma or GED required, Mechanic training required. Valid and current United States Driver’s License required. Massachusetts Hoisting License 1C/2A required. DOT Medical Certificate. Work Experience: 5 years of practical experience on repairing and maintaining small engine, gas, and diesel vehicles and equipment required. 1 year of supervisory experience in shop/garage preferred. Job Qualifications and Requirements: Physical/Environmental Demands: Prolonged periods of standing and walking. Work may be performed outside during all New England seasons. Manually lift up to 60 pounds. Heavier material or equipment is lifted using other methods. Drive a University Vehicle. Additional Requirements/Qualifications: Frequent use of verbal and written skills through the work order system. May explain and offer guidance on routine procedures. Must have an extensive understanding of gas and diesel engines. Must be able to use OBD II diagnostic computer Must be able to use hand, power and pneumatic tools and lifts Has frequent contact with other employees, professional staff and/or external contacts to give or obtain information or deliver service. Results can affect our own functional area. Must have the ability to follow instructions from supervisors or senior managers. Must be able to direct and work with other staff members. Decisions may affect a work unit or area within a department. May contribute to business and operational decisions that affect the department.   Problems are varied, requiring analysis or interpretation of the situation. Problems are solved using knowledge and skills, general precedents, and practices. Results are defined. Determines how to accomplish results with little guidance. Supervision/manager provides broad guidance and overall direction. Must be able to work within Facilities Services budget. Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Lecturer-in-History--pre-1900-US-History-_R0013686": {
      "title": "Lecturer in History (pre-1900 U.S. History)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-15\nPart time\n\nThe Department of History at Brandeis University seeks an instructor for one 4-credit course in pre-1900 U.S. History to be offered in Spring 2027. Depending on the expertise of the instructor, this course could be offered as HIST 153b Slavery and the American Civil War. Candidates with a PhD in History and training in pre-1900 U.S. History will be given preference. Interested individuals should submit a cover letter, CV, draft course syllabi, and names and contact information for two references. The deadline for first consideration is October 10. This appointment is to a position that is in a collective bargaining unit represented by SEIU Local 509.  The compensation for this course is $8764.20. Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
