    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Events-Manager_R0013561-1": {
      "title": "Events Manager",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-08\nFull time\n\nBrandeis University is seeking an Events Manager to provide operational and administrative support for a wide range of University events and programs. This is a highly detail-oriented, behind-the-scenes role focused on making sure the many logistical pieces of an event come together smoothly. The Events Manager will support institutional events and signature annual programming, as well as events organized by departments across the University. The role involves coordinating event logistics, managing details and information, supporting space and technology needs, working with campus partners, and helping ensure that established processes and requirements are followed. A significant part of the role involves working directly with students and supporting student-focused events and programs. The ideal candidate is patient, approachable and comfortable working with students who may need guidance and support throughout the event planning and execution process. This person should enjoy the operational side of events, be exceptionally organized and detail-oriented, and be able to manage a high volume of requests and competing priorities in a fast-paced environment. This is a great opportunity for someone who enjoys the behind-the-scenes work that makes events successful and takes pride in keeping things organized, anticipating needs and ensuring that details don't fall through the cracks. Application Review Applications will begin to be reviewed on September 14, 2026 . We encourage interested candidates to apply after this date as well; applications will continue to be accepted and reviewed on an ongoing basis until the position is filled. The max budget for the position is $69k. Key Responsibilities Coordinate Brandeis-affiliated events and signature annual programming as assigned, providing operational and administrative support throughout the planning and execution process. (40%) Plan, organize and manage event logistics and administrative activities , including budgets, facilities, technology, equipment, accommodations, catering and other event-related requirements. (30%) Review budgets and contractual provisions and help determine requirements related to space, location, technology, equipment, accommodations, catering and on-site management. (20%) Assess and improve event planning processes , identifying opportunities to make event coordination more efficient, consistent and effective. (5%) Perform other related duties as assigned. (5%) Qualifications Bachelor's degree required. Minimum of 2–3 years of related experience in event coordination, program support, administrative operations or a similar field; experience in a higher education or academic setting preferred. Exceptional organizational skills and attention to detail, with the ability to manage multiple requests, tasks and deadlines simultaneously. Strong interpersonal and diplomatic skills and the ability to establish effective, collaborative working relationships with students, faculty, staff and other campus partners. Patience, flexibility and a service-oriented approach , particularly when working with students and individuals with varying levels of event planning experience. Ability to provide guidance on event processes and best practices while working within established policies and procedures. Strong problem-solving skills and the ability to remain calm and resourceful when plans change or unexpected issues arise. Comfortable working in a fast-paced, highly operational environment with a significant amount of administrative and behind-the-scenes coordination. Highly proficient in Microsoft Word and Excel and comfortable working with shared documents and Google Workspace applications. Familiarity with the 25Live event and space reservation system preferred. Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Adjunct-Instructor-in-Data-Communication-in-Biotechnology--Capstone-_R0011699": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor in Data Communication in Biotechnology (Capstone)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-08\nPart time\n\nBrandeis University's Graduate Professional Studies (GPS) is looking for an industry leader to teach in our Master of Science in Applied Biotechnology & Enterprise program. Brandeis University is consistently ranked among the nation's top universities, and our online courses are developed using best practices in online learning. Information about Brandeis University and Graduate Professional Studies can be found online. Course Title: Data Communication in Biotechnology (Capstone) This capstone course provides students with the skills and tools needed to effectively communicate scientific and business-related data to diverse audiences. Emphasis is placed on storytelling with data, visualizations, scientific writing, executive summaries, and presentation skills. Students will synthesize knowledge gained throughout the program to produce and present a comprehensive data communication project. The course prepares students for real-world biotech communication scenarios including investor briefings, regulatory submissions, and internal team reports. Qualifications Qualified candidates will have Subject Matter Qualifications in the following areas. Required: - Current employment or recent experience in the biotechnology, pharma, or data science sector -  Ph.D., M.B.A. or M.S. - Experience presenting scientific or technical data to internal or external stakeholders - Expertise in communication strategies and data visualization techniques Preferred: - Prior experience teaching, coaching, or mentoring scientific professionals - Familiarity with online learning platforms and digital presentation tools General Responsibilities - Guide students through a structured capstone communication project - Deliver feedback that strengthens students’ storytelling, structure, and visuals - Lead group workshops and encourage collaboration and professional growth - Maintain the course site and facilitate timely student engagement - Mentor students through development of capstone communication projects tailored to industry or academic audiences. - Facilitate peer review and iterative feedback to refine writing, visuals, and delivery techniques. General Skill Requirements - Strong interpersonal and written communication skills - Excellent organizational and mentoring capabilities - Ability to teach storytelling with data and translate technical content for both scientific and non-scientific stakeholders. About the Master of Science in Applied Biotechnology & Enterprise This program prepares students to innovate and lead in the fast-paced biotech industry, integrating scientific knowledge with business strategy. Students learn through project-based, real-world applications. All GPS courses are 8-weeks long and taught asynchronously online. About GPS Faculty GPS Faculty are active industry professionals who teach part-time, online. Our instructors hold at least a master's degree, and many have terminal degrees and professional certifications. Previous teaching experience is not required; GPS provides full training. Application Process: Interested candidates should submit: A cover letter highlighting relevant qualifications and teaching experience. A current CV or resume. Contact information for three professional references. (Optional) Examples of teaching materials or professional publications related to leadership and industry innovation. This appointment is to a position that is in a collective bargaining unit represented by SEIU Local 509.  Compensation for this position is $6573.15 Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Adjunct-Instructor-in-Commercial-Operations-in-Biotechnology_R0011700": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor in Commercial Operations in Biotechnology",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-08\nPart time\n\nBrandeis University's Graduate Professional Studies (GPS) is looking for an industry leader to teach in our Master of Science in Applied Biotechnology & Enterprise program. Brandeis University is consistently ranked among the nation's top universities, and our online courses are developed using best practices in online learning. Information about Brandeis University and Graduate Professional Studies can be found online. Course Title: Commercial Operations in Biotechnology This course provides an overview of commercial strategy in the biotech sector, covering market access, sales operations, product launch planning, and pricing strategies. Students will learn how cross-functional commercial teams work with R&D and regulatory departments to bring biotech products to market successfully. Qualifications Qualified candidates will have Subject Matter Qualifications in the following areas. Required: - Current active employment in the relevant biotech or pharmaceutical industry - Deep knowledge of course-specific applications and industry practices - Ph.D., M.D., or M.B.A. - Strong communication and analytical skills Preferred: - Previous teaching experience or experience delivering professional training - Familiarity with online instructional tools and platforms General Responsibilities - Design a syllabus following program chair guidance and the syllabus template - Create content aligned with course outcomes, integrating personal expertise - Facilitate online discussions and provide timely, relevant feedback - Maintain the course site and update content in the Moodle LMS - Provide tools and templates for market analysis, forecasting, and sales planning. - Lead simulations of product launches and go-to-market strategy execution. General Skill Requirements - Strong interpersonal skills and the ability to foster student engagement - Written communication skills, especially for online learning - Ability to manage time effectively and respond promptly to students - Deep understanding of biotech sales operations, product marketing, and market access strategies. About the Master of Science in Applied Biotechnology & Enterprise This program prepares students to innovate and lead in the fast-paced biotech industry, integrating scientific knowledge with business strategy. Students learn through project-based, real-world applications. All GPS courses are 8-weeks long and taught asynchronously online. About GPS Faculty GPS Faculty are active industry professionals who teach part-time, online. Our instructors hold at least a master's degree, and many have terminal degrees and professional certifications. Previous teaching experience is not required; GPS provides full training. Application Process: Interested candidates should submit: A cover letter highlighting relevant qualifications and teaching experience. A current CV or resume. Contact information for three professional references. (Optional) Examples of teaching materials or professional publications related to leadership and industry innovation. This appointment is to a position that is in a collective bargaining unit represented by SEIU Local 509.  Compensation for this position is $6573.15 Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Adjunct-Instructor-in-Artificial-Intelligence---Machine-Learning-in-Drug-Development_R0011702-1": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor in Artificial Intelligence & Machine Learning in Drug Development",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-08\nPart time\n\nBrandeis University's Graduate Professional Studies (GPS) is looking for an industry leader to teach in our Master of Science in Applied Biotechnology & Enterprise program. Brandeis University is consistently ranked among the nation's top universities, and our online courses are developed using best practices in online learning. Information about Brandeis University and Graduate Professional Studies can be found online. Course Title: Artificial Intelligence & Machine Learning in Drug Development This course explores the use of AI and ML in pharmaceutical R&D. Topics include data preprocessing, predictive modeling, and applications in target identification, drug screening, and clinical trial optimization. Students gain hands-on experience using tools and algorithms to analyze real-world biotech data sets. Qualifications Qualified candidates will have Subject Matter Qualifications in the following areas. Required: - Current active employment in the relevant biotech or pharmaceutical industry - Deep knowledge of course-specific applications and industry practices - Ph.D. or M.S. - Strong communication and analytical skills Preferred: - Previous teaching experience or experience delivering professional training - Familiarity with online instructional tools and platforms General Responsibilities - Design a syllabus following program chair guidance and the syllabus template - Create content aligned with course outcomes, integrating personal expertise - Facilitate online discussions and provide timely, relevant feedback - Maintain the course site and update content in the Moodle LMS - Lead students in AI model development and interpretation using biotech case studies. - Help students critically assess the limitations and ethics of AI in pharmaceutical innovation. General Skill Requirements - Strong interpersonal skills and the ability to foster student engagement - Written communication skills, especially for online learning - Ability to manage time effectively and respond promptly to students - Proficiency in applying machine learning tools (e.g., Python, scikit-learn, TensorFlow) to drug discovery datasets. About the Master of Science in Applied Biotechnology & Enterprise This program prepares students to innovate and lead in the fast-paced biotech industry, integrating scientific knowledge with business strategy. Students learn through project-based, real-world applications. All GPS courses are 8-weeks long and taught asynchronously online. About GPS Faculty GPS Faculty are active industry professionals who teach part-time, online. Our instructors hold at least a master's degree, and many have terminal degrees and professional certifications. Previous teaching experience is not required; GPS provides full training. Application Process: Interested candidates should submit: A cover letter highlighting relevant qualifications and teaching experience. A current CV or resume. Contact information for three professional references. (Optional) Examples of teaching materials or professional publications related to leadership and industry innovation. This appointment is to a position that is in a collective bargaining unit represented by SEIU Local 509.  The compensation for this position is $6573.15 Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Adjunct-Instructor-in-Regulatory-Science---Quality-Control_R0011428": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor in Regulatory Science & Quality Control",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-08\nPart time\n\nBrandeis University's Graduate Professional Studies (GPS) is looking for an industry leader to teach in our Master of Science in Applied Biotechnology & Enterprise program. Brandeis University is consistently ranked among the nation's top universities, and our online courses are developed using best practices in online learning.  About the Position and Course  Course Title: Regulatory Science & Quality Control  This course provides an in-depth examination of regulatory frameworks governing biotechnology products, including FDA, EMA, and ICH guidelines. Topics include drug approval processes, quality control strategies, risk assessment, and post-market surveillance. Students will develop a strong understanding of regulatory compliance through hands-on assignments and real-world industry case studies. Qualifications Qualified candidates will have subject matter expertise in the following areas.  Required:  - Current active employment in the biotechnology, pharmaceutical, or life sciences industry - Strong knowledge of the course topic, including industry applications and emerging trends - Ph.D. in a relevant field  - Experience with hands-on industry applications related to the course content  Preferred:  - Previous teaching experience at the college level or experience conducting professional training - Experience in online education, instructional design, or curriculum development - Familiarity with computational tools and data analysis relevant to the subject matter General Responsibilities - Develop and deliver course content that aligns with Brandeis teaching standards. - Facilitate discussions, provide timely feedback, and grade assignments.  Additional Responsibilities:  - Teach students how to prepare and submit regulatory documentation for biotech products. - Provide insights into quality assurance strategies for maintaining compliance in biomanufacturing. General Skill Requirements - Strong communication and teaching skills in an online environment. - Strong attention to detail in regulatory documentation and compliance monitoring. About the Master of Science in Applied Biotechnology & Enterprise Biotechnology is an evolving field that integrates scientific advancements with business applications to drive innovation in healthcare, agriculture, and environmental sustainability. This online master's program is designed to provide students with both the technical expertise and commercial acumen necessary to lead in the biotech industry. All GPS master's courses are 8 weeks long and taught asynchronously in the online learning environment, with no set days or times for interaction. About GPS Faculty GPS Faculty members are active practitioners in the industries that align with our programs and bring their professional expertise into course discussions and learning activities. Instructors work part-time, fully online, with no requirement to appear on campus. Our faculty hold at least a master’s degree, with many possessing terminal academic degrees and industry-specific certifications. Previous online teaching experience is not required; GPS offers a comprehensive training program for qualified applicants. This appointment is to a position that is in a collective bargaining unit represented by SEIU Local 509.  Compensation for this position is $6573.15 Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Temp-Course-Assisnts-Graders_R0013621-1": {
      "title": "Temp Course Assistants/Graders",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-02\nPart time\n\nSeveral Course Assistant and Graders positions are available. Course Assistant duties may include but are not limited to assisting the professor with: course preparation before and during the semester; Latte maintenance; grading essays exams and homework; syllabus design; writing exam questions. Course Assistants may be required to attend classes on a regular basis. Course Assistants may also be responsible for some instructional duties including the following: lead discussion sessions, science labs, and recitations and/or teach an occasional class. They may also be responsible for some student advising and may be required to hold office hours. Qualifications: Course Assistants must have successfully completed graduate-level coursework in the area to which he or she is assigned. Must have achieved high grades in the same class they are assisting in (or an equivalent course as determined by instructor or professor). Excellent interpersonal, writing, and communication skills. Must demonstrate a high level of critical thinking and analysis. Must demonstrate attention to detail and strong time-management capabilities.   Course Assistant will be paid a total of $3,200 Grader will be paid a total of $2,500 Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Senior-Assistant-Director--Financial-Aid_R0013590": {
      "title": "Senior Assistant Director, Financial Aid",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-31\nFull time\n\nSenior Assistant Director, Financial Aid | Brandeis University | Waltham, MA Hiring Range: $74k - $76k Exciting opportunity available supporting the Senior Associate Director, Financial Aid . About the Role: This position will review financial aid applications and awards assistance in accordance with federal, state, and institutional policies. Responsible for determining student eligibility using both Federal and Institutional Methodologies, performing verifications, evaluating appeals, and maintaining accurate financial aid records. Counsels’ students and families on all aspects of the financial aid lifecycle and manages one or more specialized programs. Key Responsibilities: Performs need analysis using both Institutional and Federal Methodologies for incoming and returning students. Awards institutional and federal grants, loans, scholarships, and work study in alignment with university packaging policies and federal regulations. Conducts file verification for matriculated and returning students by analyzing tax returns and all other required documentation. Provides comprehensive counseling to students and families regarding financial aid awards, alternative financing options, special circumstances, and loan repayment, deferment and consolidation. Prepares and presents student appeals to the Appeals Committee, modifying aid packages to reflect committee determinations. Provides leadership to Assistant Directors and serves as a point of contact in the absence of Senior Associate Directors and Director of Financial Aid. One or more of the following program responsibilities as assigned, including but not limited to: FAFSA Processing, Federal Pell Grant Processing, Direct Loan Processing, MA/VT/PA State Scholarship Processing, restricted and unrestricted endowed scholarship assignments, Study Abroad, Summer Aid, Graduate Aid Liaison, Compliance Assistant, CA Stipends, Tuition Remissions, Tuition Exchange Other Duties as Required Education: B.A./B.S./equivalent required. Experience: 3-5 years required; 5+ years preferred. Core Competencies: Must have strong interpersonal skills and ability to work independently and as part of a team. Direct experience in the delivery and administration of student financial aid preferred. Experience using PowerFaids preferred.  Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Associate-University-Librarian--Archives-and-Special-Collections_R0013558-2": {
      "title": "Associate University Librarian, Archives and Special Collections",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-31\nFull time\n\nBrandeis University is looking for an Associate University Librarian, Archives and Special Collections . Reporting to the University Librarian, this position provides strategic direction, development, and executing vision for the University Archives & Special Collections Department. Responsible for the development, administration, operations, and coordination of the areas of University Archives, Special Collections, University Records Management, and Preservation. Directs approx. 7 staff responsible for engaging with the campus and larger scholarly communities to advance the academic and institutional mission of the university. Supervises staff teaching in class settings at the undergraduate and graduate levels. Works closely with institutional advancement to cultivate donors and apply for grants. Contributes as a member of the senior administrative team toward library-wide planning and the shaping of strategic directions for the Library. Job Description: Provides strong leadership, vision, and strategic direction in the development, delivery, and integration of new and existing programs and projects that advance progress toward the department and Library’s goals. Oversees the development of policies for the department’s operations and services, including the management of rights and permissions regarding copyright-protected and confidential information. Represents the department and the Library on appropriate committees and at external and internal professional meetings, fundraising events, etc. Coordinates activities, services, and events with other units and departments within the Library and across the University.   Represents the department and the Library on appropriate committees and at external and internal professional meetings, fundraising events, etc. Coordinates closely with Institutional Advancement for fundraising and grant opportunities. Oversees the acquisition of archival and special collections materials. Manages and administers the Archives & Special Collections budget. Leads and directs the planning and development of the University Archives & Special Collections Department. Interacts with donors; applies for and administers grants supporting historical collections and digitization projects. Oversees the development of policies for the department’s operations and services, including the management of rights and permissions regarding copyright protected and confidential information.   Leads and directs department staff, including in the areas of University Archives, Special Collections, University Records Management, Judaica, and Preservation. Directs staff who oversee the arrangement and description, cataloging, and provision of access (paper and electronic) to archival, manuscript, and special collections. Leads and directs staff who develop strategies for and implement the digitization of selected archival, manuscript, institutional, and special collections. Oversees the provision of reference, instructional, and outreach services to the Brandeis and broader research communities. Supervises staff teaching in class settings at the undergraduate and graduate levels.    Assists in the administration and management of the Brandeis Library. Contributes as a senior staff member to Library strategic planning, management, development, and administration. The salary range for the position is $119k-$128k. For priority consideration, please submit your application by September 25, 2026. The position will remain open until filled. Additional Information: End expertise across analog and digital collections including bibliographic data, content management and digital preservation systems, web archiving, and emerging tools. This is a full-time, on-campus position with the possibility of occasional remote work. Occasionally work in areas prone to dust or mold with appropriate personal protective equipment. Job Requirements: A terminal professional degree, preferably in library or archival studies (for example, in the United States, a Master's degree in library/information science) is required. Over 8 years of total work-related experience, including 3-5 years of supervisory/management experience. Significant experience in a research library, including strategic planning and budgeting. Experience in collection development and fund management. The Associate University Librarian is primarily an on-site position.  Experience leveraging consortium partnerships. Demonstrated knowledge of best practices and current trends in both digital and physical resource management and operations. Demonstrated success in: Leading and managing organizational change. Collaboration and strategic partnering. Experience in designing and coordinating transformational initiatives and bringing them to timely and successful implementation. Creative integration of automated and manual technical services processes preferred. Excellent interpersonal and communication skills, including the ability to be an effective spokesperson and presenter. Demonstrated ability to work successfully in diverse environments and to collaborate with people from varied professional, cultural, and educational backgrounds. Commitment to contributing to an environment where all members of the community can access resources, feel respected, and thrive. Record of professional contribution and engagement. This position manages approximately six members of the Library’s collective bargaining unit, represented by the Service Employees International Union (SEIU) Local 888, for purposes of wages, hours, and other terms and conditions of employment. Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Philanthropy-Fellow-at-The-Blue-Square-Alliance-Against-Hate--BSA-_R0013581-1": {
      "title": "Philanthropy Fellow at The Blue Square Alliance Against Hate (BSA)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-31\nFull time\n\nPlay a critical role in the fight against Jewish hate. As part of the Robert Kraft Family-Brandeis Initiative on Antisemitism, this 1 to 2-year fellowship offers any recent Brandeis graduate an opportunity to work with the Blue Square Alliance Against Hate (BSA). BSA launched the Stand Up To Jewish Hate campaign in 2023, reaching millions of people across the United States and establishing the Blue Square as a universal symbol to stand against antisemitism and all forms of hate. Through powerful positive messaging and partnerships with prominent Jewish and non-Jewish organizations, BSA is strongly positioned to drive effective change. BSA leverages unique social media monitoring technology to drive effective rapid response to Jewish hate and all hate, in addition to proactive programs that galvanize popular support for the Jewish community. The Blue Square Alliance Against Hate aims to inspire and mobilize all people to become upstanders against Jewish hate. Philanthropy Fellow The Philanthropy Fellow will support BSA’s fundraising, strategy and engagement efforts. This role is designed for individuals passionate about antisemitism, philanthropy, and nonprofit engagement, offering a unique opportunity to gain hands-on experience and make meaningful contributions to BSA’s philanthropic initiatives. Key areas of focus will include donor stewardship and engagement, prospect research, and data management. This fellowship provides an opportunity for professional development and offers a pathway for future careers in fields including but not limited to fundraising, sales, nonprofit management, and business development. This position reports to Maya Buki Dabby, Chief Philanthropy Officer. Minimum Qualifications: The successful candidate for this role would be a recent Brandeis undergraduate with a keen eye for detail and strong research skills. We welcome all majors to apply. They should possess excellent communication abilities, both written and verbal, and be proficient in using tools including Microsoft Office. Training will be provided for Salesforce and other tools. Curiosity, creativity, and a commitment to making a difference through their work is essential in this position. Key areas of focus will include donor engagement and stewardship, research and data management, and grant writing and proposal development. Key Responsibilities: Research and Data Management (50%): Conduct in-depth research on potential and existing prospects, focusing on wealth screenings, philanthropic giving patterns, and personal interests to tailor engagement strategies. Maintain accurate donor records in Salesforce to assist the philanthropy team on reporting progress towards fundraising goals. Support the philanthropy team in timely completion of tasks and reporting. Grant Writing and Proposal Development (30%): Support the development of grant proposals for institution funding and private foundations. Assist with reporting to and renewing grant-based gifts and ensuring all requirements are met. Donor Engagement and Stewardship (20%): Assist in the creation and execution of donor stewardship plans for key donors, ensuring recognition that aligns with each donor’s level of giving. Monitor and track all donor interactions including drafting acknowledgement letters, letters, and additional stewardship materials for major donors. Salary: This position works in person at the BSA offices at Gillette Stadium! Salary range is $50,000 - $55,000. Benefits: This is an exempt, benefits-eligible role working 35 hours/week. As a Brandeis employee, you'll be eligible for generous employee benefits. You’ll also gain valuable experience in research, analysis, and communication along with mentoring and support from a dedicated team. You can make a tangible impact by contributing to the fight against antisemitism. This is a one year position with the possibility of an extension for an additional year. Applications: Please submit a resume and cover letter. The first review of applications is in early September, and the expected start time frame is October. Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Workday-Analyst_R0013564": {
      "title": "Workday Analyst",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-26\nFull time\n\nBrandeis University is looking for a Workday Analyst to work in the IT Department. The Workday ITS Analyst participates in the ongoing enhancements, configuration, object maintenance, reporting, and data integrity for Workday. This position is a hands-on technical and functional role and is responsible for supporting the delivery of solutions across all modules in the Workday platform (HCM, Finance, and Student). The Workday ITS Analyst will participate in the ongoing enhancements, configuration, object maintenance, reporting, and data integrity for Workday. This position is a hands-on technical and functional role and is responsible for supporting the delivery of solutions across all modules in the Workday platform (HCM, Finance, and Student). Collaborates with functional users to analyze work process design and flow, improve processes and leverage the return on technological capabilities. This position serves as a liaison between university departments and Information Technology Services to provide systems support and analysis, and to leverage technology solutions to meet the needs of the users of Workday throughout the University. The Workday ITS Analyst collaborates with ITS Project Managers in building project plans, adheres to project schedules, and works effectively with peers. Job Description: - The Workday ITS Analyst will play a key role in the ongoing maintenance and enhancement of Workday to meet changing business requirements to improve efficiency and service. Prepare for and support twice-annual Workday systems upgrades, releases, and annual processes. Recommends process/customer service improvements, innovative solutions, which must be approved by appropriate governance bodies prior to implementation. Diagnose and resolve system issues (e.g. end-user, business process, security, integration, etc….). Under the direction of the Sr. Assoc. Director of ERP Operations and Implementations will assist with leading defined scope projects and new functionality implementations by defining implementation requirements and completing projects in an accurate, efficient, and timely manner. Utilizes interpersonal skills when working with various customers and peers to accomplish project goals. Assists in the testing for Workday semi-annual releases, including the development of test plans and other supporting documentation (i.e. scripts). Review release notes, assess new features and impacts to business processes and basic changes in functionality. Assists with coordinating project activities, such as prototyping, testing, communication, and rollout with University departments as needed. - Responsible for investigating, analyzing and resolving end-user problems and Workday related systems issues through configuration analysis, process improvement, data loading and data integrity, reporting, research, testing, and/or scenario re-creation. - Assists with Workday report requirements gathering and development as needed. - Maintains awareness of current trends in Workday with a focus on product and service development, delivery and support, and applying key technologies. Participates actively in the Workday Community. Participates in user group meetings/conferences. The salary range for the position is $85k - 93K. Job Requirements: Education: Bachelor’s degree in Computer Science, Engineering, or related discipline, or equivalent HRIS work experience. Work Experience:   2 years experience configuring and maintaining business processes, including creating condition rules. Basic knowledge of Workday security and general knowledge of Workday integration concepts. Experience in maintaining a high degree of discretion with data . Technical Skills:  Experience with Workday request framework, business processes, custom object forms, and questionnaires. Experience creating Workday custom objects. Experience with Workday reporting writing required.  Functional Skills: Functional knowledge of HR and Finance core business processes, as well as a strong background in systems analysis and support, with a demonstrated ability to learn and apply new technologies to solve business challenges, is required. Knowledge of HCM and Finance concepts, practices, and procedures, with the ability to apply them in routine situations and the ability to innovate. Functional knowledge of student information systems is a plus. Problem-Solving Skills : Strong analytical and troubleshooting skills with the ability to resolve complex integration issues effectively. Demonstrated ability to learn and apply new technologies to solve business challenges and the ability to innovate and implement creative solutions to optimize Workday’s functionality. Ability to handle multiple high-priority projects simultaneously. Ability to work both independently and collaboratively in a professional manner with an emphasis on service quality. Project Management: Experience working in cross-functional project teams and handling multiple integrations and applications simultaneously. Communication Skills : Excellent verbal and written communication skills to convey technical concepts to non-technical stakeholders. Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Workday-Integrations---Applications-Developer_R0013565-1": {
      "title": "Workday Integrations & Applications Developer",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-26\nFull time\n\nBrandeis University is looking to hire a Workday Integrations and Applications Developer in the IT Department. The Workday Integrations Developer uses in-depth knowledge of Workday's technical and functional capabilities to design, develop, and implement solutions across all modules in the Workday platform (HCM, Finance, Student, PRISM, and Extend). Collaborates with functional users to improve business processes by identifying opportunities for increased efficiency and effectiveness, and evaluates new Workday features and modules to determine potential use cases and return on investment. Functions as a technical expert on integration and application projects involving Workday, third-party systems, and enterprise data. Contributes to the discussions regarding the Workday technical roadmap for Brandeis, and provides technology guidance on Workday technical design and development to other members of the ITS Workday team. The salary range for the position is $82.9 - 89.7k Job Description: Workday Integration and Application Development: (60%) Design and Development: Create and maintain custom integrations and applications using Workday Studio, Enterprise Interface Builder (EIB), Core Connectors, Workday Web Services (SOAP/REST), Extend, and other Workday tools to support business requirements. Provide estimates and initial assessment for proposed development tasks and projects. Requirements Gathering: Collaborate with stakeholders, including HR, Finance, and Student teams, to understand integration requirements and translate them into technical specifications. Develop prototypes and review functionality and business logic with customers and business analysts. Testing and Deployment: Conduct unit tests, system tests, and end-to-end testing on integrations and applications to ensure accuracy and reliability before deployment. Support Workday updates and releases, performing thorough testing and validation to ensure smooth adoption of new features. Documentation: Create and maintain comprehensive technical documentation, including integration design, testing scripts, and troubleshooting procedures. Workday Operations: (40%) Integration/Application Maintenance: Monitor, maintain, and troubleshoot existing Workday integrations and applications to ensure they perform optimally and meet evolving business needs. Compliance and Security: Ensure data security and compliance standards are met in all integrations and applications, following internal and external regulations. Collaboration: Work closely with internal partners and vendors to align integration solutions with organizational objectives and best practices. Requirements: Education: Bachelor’s degree in Computer Science, Engineering, or related discipline, or equivalent work experience. Work Experience: 2 years as a Workday Developer with proven success building reports, Studio Integrations, EIBs, Core Connectors, and Web Services. Experience with Workday Extend is preferred. Technical Skills: Thorough understanding of software development life cycles, testing methodologies, and requirements gathering. Familiarity with product lifecycle management. Experience with Boomi middleware is a plus. Experience with Workday Reporting Writing is required. Problem-Solving Skills : Strong analytical and troubleshooting skills with the ability to resolve complex integration issues effectively. Demonstrated ability to learn and apply new technologies to solve business challenges, and the ability to innovate and implement creative solutions to optimize Workday’s functionality. Project Management: Experience working in cross-functional project teams and handling multiple integrations and applications simultaneously. Communication Skills : Excellent verbal and written communication skills to convey technical concepts to non-technical stakeholders. Additional Requirements: Understanding of higher education core business processes is preferred. Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Temp-Course-Assistant--Science--Engineering--and-Technology-_R0013529": {
      "title": "Temp Course Assistant (Science, Engineering, and Technology)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-25\nPart time\n\nSeveral Course Assistant and Graders positions are available for the School of Science, Engineering, and Technology. Course Assistant duties may include but are not limited to assisting the professor with: course preparation before and during the semester; Latte maintenance; grading essays exams and homework; syllabus design; writing exam questions. Course Assistants may be required to attend classes on a regular basis. Course Assistants may also be responsible for some instructional duties including the following: lead discussion sessions, science labs, and recitations and/or teach an occasional class. They may also be responsible for some student advising and may be required to hold office hours. Qualifications: Course Assistants must have successfully completed graduate-level coursework in the area to which he or she is assigned. Must have achieved high grades in the same class they are assisting in (or an equivalent course as determined by instructor or professor). Excellent interpersonal, writing, and communication skills. Must demonstrate a high level of critical thinking and analysis. Must demonstrate attention to detail and strong time-management capabilities.   Course Assistant will be paid a total of $3,200 Grader will be paid a total of $2,500 Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/HVAC-I_R0013423-1": {
      "title": "HVAC I",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-21\nPart time\n\nUnder the direction of the HVAC Supervisor, the Refrigeration Technician I installs, troubleshoots, performs preventative maintenance, and repairs HVAC equipment and distribution systems involving work of a varied nature with minimum supervision. This work is accomplished throughout the 100 buildings and 235 acres of Brandeis’ Campus. Must have extensive knowledge of codes regarding installation of HVAC/R systems, refrigeration, and associated equipment. Must have a minimum of five years’ experience in the general maintenance field and be able to demonstrate knowledge and understanding of basic maintenance skills. This position is a Local 615, 32BJ Service Employees International Union (SEIU) position. The hourly rate for this position is $47.29. This is a 40-hour work week, Monday - Friday, 7:00 AM - 3:00 PM Refrigeration Technician I Functions Relays information, assignments and schedules to others as directed by supervisor or manager. Relays status/progress of on-going work from other shop employees to supervisors/management on a regular basis, as defined by management. Assists with work distribution and prioritization when necessary. Trains, orients and instructs other staff as directed. Acts as a liaison between other union staff and supervisors/management to facilitate and ensure good communications. Collaborate and consults with contractors and/or Campus Staff as needed. Attend leadership meetings as needed. Obtain required permits when necessary. Main Functions Report any issues to the appropriate supervisor. Must have sufficient knowledge of HVAC, plumbing, and electrical equipment to make minor repairs and adjustments. This includes pneumatic, electrical and D.D.C. Type control systems. Installs, maintains, troubleshoots and makes repairs to HVAC equipment and systems including controls, bearings, filters, compressors, belts, motors, etc. Extensive knowledge of and use of computers regarding Building Management Systems (BMS). Operate, maintain and repair all aspects of laboratory equipment directly connected to the various HVAC systems such as refrigeration, air, gas, vacuum, hoods, etc. Keep records, write reports and identify HVAC materials. Work from blueprints, drawings, sketches and/or verbal instructions to plan, layout and install piping and equipment. Make standard shop computations relating to pressure, flow and specifications required. Perform standard tests to determine whether equipment meet specifications. Make major and minor repairs to Heating, Ventilation, Air conditioning and Refrigeration equipment in and around campus facilities. Perform preventative maintenance on all equipment. Make repairs on various types of pumps, valves and pressure regulators of various sizes. Shape, cut, ream, caulk, sweat, solder and thread a variety of piping sizes and types and related fittings using various hand tools and power-driven machines. Operate, maintain, troubleshoot and repair HVAC and mechanical equipment. Work from blueprints, drawings, sketches and/or verbal instructions to plan, layout and install equipment. Must be able to work from ladders, bucket trucks, scaffolds, pits, and manholes. Installs appliances and equipment as needed. Must be able to work alone with minimal supervision. Provides appropriate support during events, annual electrical shutdown, opening of school and commencement. General Functions Follow proper safety procedures and observe all safety rules and regulations including OSHA. Reports building damage, malfunctioning equipment, needed maintenance and /or repairs in assigned work areas. Respond to emergency calls-ins as needed. Read, write, understand, and carry out oral and written instructions in English. Keeps logs and write shift reports. Assess and update all work order assignments using the department's computerized work order management system. Keeps logs, records time, writes reports (including shift reports), and identifies/requests materials, promptly using the management system and the stock room. Participates in training to keep and acquire knowledge, skills, and abilities in order to remain current with standard operational practices and revised standards. Establish and maintain a positive cooperative working relationship with supervisors, fellow workers, department heads, and other members of the campus community. Work in an environment subject to frequent changing of priorities and time deadlines. Drive a University Vehicle. *This list is representative of those duties and responsibilities that are required of this position. However, the list is not to be considered as all inclusive. Job Qualifications and Requirements Physical/Environmental Demands: Prolonged periods of standing and walking. Must be physically able to climb ladders, bend, or crawl into awkward spaces. Must work on ladders, bucks trucks, scaffolds, and pits. Manually lift up to 60 pounds. Heavier material or equipment is lifted using other methods. Work inside and outside all campus buildings and grounds; including on rooftops, in vaults, and outside throughout campus property. Work will be performed in and outside during all New England seasons. Education, Work and License Requirements: High School Diploma or GED required, vocational or technical training required. Valid and current United States Driver’s License required. Valid Massachusetts Refrigeration Technicians License required. EPA Universal Certification required. Valid Massachusetts Refrigeration Contractors License required. OSHA 10 training preferred. Work Experience: 5 years’ total work-related experience. Additional Requirements/Qualifications: Frequent use of verbal and written skills through the work order system. May explain and offer guidance on routine procedures. Must have a good working knowledge of plumbing, electrical, HVAC systems, and basic carpentry in order to address minor repairs and adjustments on their own. Must be able to use hand and power tools. Has frequent contact with other employees, professional staff and/or external contacts to give or obtain information or delivery service. Results can affect own functional area. Must have the ability to follow instructions from supervisors or senior managers. Decisions may affect a work unit or area within a department. May contribute to business and operational decisions that affect the department. Problems are varied, requiring analysis or interpretation of the situation. Problems are solved using knowledge and skills, general precedents, and practices. Results are defined. Determines how to accomplish results with little guidance. Supervision/manager provides broad guidance and overall direction. Must be able to work within Facilities Services budget. Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Supervisor--Custodial-Services_R0012791-1": {
      "title": "Supervisor, Custodial Services",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-19\nFull time\n\nBrandeis University is delighted to announce a career opportunity as the Supervisor Custodial Services in the Facilities Department. At Brandeis, we offer competitive benefits and compensation package which includes medical, dental and life insurance.  If you are looking to advance your career through educational opportunities, Brandeis offers tuition assistance for employees, dependents and spouses.  Our 403b retirement savings plan includes a generous match. The Supervisor, Custodial Services supervises custodial staff engaged in the cleaning and maintenance of university buildings and other related custodial services programs. May work with a special emphasis on after hours including evenings and weekends. Assures that assigned areas of campus facilities are orderly, safe from snow and ice hazards, and properly cleaned and maintained. May supervise the preparations of buildings for special events. Responsible for interviewing prospective employees and recommending the selection of qualified candidates. Responsible for properly training custodial staff. Reviews performance of staff and procedures to drive continued improvement. Expected to maintain a productive working relationship with faculty, students, staff, contractors and visitors to the university. The hiring range for this position is $69.8k - $79.5k. Schedule is Monday – Friday, 5:00 AM – 1:00 PM, this is a 35 hour work week. Responsibilities: Supervises and directs custodians, including head custodians, in the effective cleaning and maintaining of university buildings, including preparing facilities for special events. Additionally, actively involved in interviewing and recommending prospective employees. Inspects buildings on a regular schedule. Coordinates overtime to ensure proper coverage. Coordinates with customers on all matters relating to services requested, by both in house staff and outside vendors and contractors. May communicate with other departmental supervisors and staff as necessary with regard to emergencies or other incidents that occur on the evening and weekend shifts. Assists in the development and implementation of work methods and procedures designed to achieve cost effective, efficient operations. Trains personnel in the performance of their prescribed duties.  Participates in ongoing supervisory skill development and maintains up-to-date knowledge of technical and safety practices. Administers work orders for general repair/replacement e.g. furniture, bathrooms fixtures within the university buildings. May assist with campus wide scheduling and billing for moves and carpet cleaning. Monitors, orders, and distributes supplies and equipment to custodial staff. Prepare written reports and other correspondence as needed. May coordinate first response efforts to respond to emergencies, access situations, and coordinate crews in the process of clean up. Additional Job Description: Drive a university vehicle. Work environment may be subject to temperature extremes of hot or cold. Job requires contact with abrasive cleaning chemicals/hazards. Requires proficiency in spoken and written English. Education, Work Experience and Licenses: Education: High School diploma or GED required. BA/BS with technical training preferred. Work Experience: 5-8 years. Valid and current MA State driver's license required. Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Senior-Assistant-Director--Assistant-Director-of-Employer-and-Alumni-Engagement_R0013451": {
      "title": "Senior Assistant Director /Assistant Director of Employer and Alumni Engagement",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-18\nFull time\n\nBrandeis University Center for Careers and Applied Liberal Arts Assistant Director, Employer and Alumni Engagement/ Sr. Assistant Director, Employer and Alumni Engagement Shape the Future of Career-Ready Education Brandeis University seeks to hire an Assistant Director/Sr. Assistant Director in the Center for Careers and Applied Liberal Arts. In this position, you would build and expand a network of employers and alumni on behalf of the Center, developing relationships and partnerships that open doors, including recruitment and experiential learning opportunities, for Brandeis students.  Multiple positions are available. Title and salary commensurate with experience and qualifications. The hiring range for this position is: Assistant Director, Employer and Alumni Engagement - $72,900 - $78,300 Sr. Assistant Director, Employer and Alumni Engagement - $80,200 - $86,100 Why this role matters You'll serve as the primary employer and alumni engagement lead for one or more Brandeis schools, working closely with colleagues across the university to develop engagement strategies aligned with each school's academic strengths and career pathways. Through proactive outreach, relationship building, and program development, you'll cultivate employer and alumni partnerships that create meaningful opportunities for students. You'll also design and deliver recruiting programs, including industry treks, industry nights, and information sessions, that strengthen employer engagement and raise Brandeis's visibility across key industries and markets. This role is ideal for someone who enjoys building strong relationships, thinks strategically about partnerships, and is energized by creating new opportunities through collaboration with employers and alumni. Job Duties Employer and Alumni Outreach and Development (50%) Serve as the primary employer and alumni engagement lead for one or more schools, developing a working knowledge of the assigned school's academic programs, student populations, and career paths Initiate and deepen relationships with employers and alumni through targeted outreach, expanding strategic partnerships that create opportunities for Brandeis students. Own a portfolio of priority employer accounts, developing an engagement plan for each that grows the relationship from single touchpoints into sustained, multi-channel partnerships Serve as a strategic advisor to employers, recommending recruiting approaches, campus engagement options, and timing that fit their hiring needs and improve their results with Brandeis students Identify and open new industries and geographic markets for Brandeis recruiting, based on student career interests and market analysis Use Handshake, LinkedIn, and alumni data to identify and prioritize target employers and alumni within the industries most relevant to the assigned school Travel to employer sites and professional events to build relationships and represent Brandeis Develop and customize employer outreach materials including presentations, one-pagers, and targeted communications Programming and Events (35%) Collaborate with career services colleagues to design and execute the Industry Trek program, connecting students with employers through company visits and alumni engagement.  Plan and execute recruiting-focused programs and events, including industry nights and information sessions, that connect employers and alumni with students Collaborate with colleagues to design programs and strategies that work across schools Professional Development and Operations (15%) Participate in professional development activities relevant to employer relations, including regional events, associations and/or conferences Maintain accurate, up-to-date records of all employer and alumni contacts and interactions in Handshake and other tracking systems Perform other duties as assigned What We're Looking For Bachelor's degree required 3-7 years experience required Experience in employer relations, recruiting or a related field; backgrounds in business development or account management are also welcome; higher education experience is helpful but not required Demonstrated ability to initiate relationships through proactive outreach and build them into lasting, productive partnerships Ability to research industries, identify target organizations and tailor outreach strategies to align with institutional and student needs Comfortable representing Brandeis in person at employer sites, regional events and professional conferences Strong organizational skills, including consistent use of CRM tools and tracking systems; familiarity with Handshake or similar career management platforms a plus Clear, professional communicator in writing, by phone and in person Please submit a cover letter with your application for consideration. You should upload the letter with your resume; there is not a separate prompt for a cover letter in the candidate application system. Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Temp-Course-Assistants-Graders_R0013405-1": {
      "title": "Temp Course Assistants/Graders",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-13\nPart time\n\nSeveral Course Assistant and Graders positions are available. Course Assistant duties may include but are not limited to assisting the professor with: course preparation before and during the semester; Latte maintenance; grading essays exams and homework; syllabus design; writing exam questions. Course Assistants may be required to attend classes on a regular basis. Course Assistants may also be responsible for some instructional duties including the following: lead discussion sessions, science labs, and recitations and/or teach an occasional class. They may also be responsible for some student advising and may be required to hold office hours. Qualifications: Course Assistants must have successfully completed graduate-level coursework in the area to which he or she is assigned. Must have achieved high grades in the same class they are assisting in (or an equivalent course as determined by instructor or professor). Excellent interpersonal, writing, and communication skills. Must demonstrate a high level of critical thinking and analysis. Must demonstrate attention to detail and strong time-management capabilities.   Course Assistant will be paid a total of $3,200 Grader will be paid a total of $2,500 Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Web-Writer-and-Content-Specialist_R0013430-1": {
      "title": "Web Writer and Content Specialist",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-12\nFull time\n\nThe Brandeis Office of Marketing Communications seeks a Web Writer and Content Specialist to join its digital team. Reporting to the Director of Marketing Technology, with close collaboration with the editorial team, this role is responsible for creating, editing and publishing high-quality, user-centered content across Brandeis' web properties and digital channels. This role will also contribute to an upcoming website redesign, with opportunities to help inform content direction and standards. The ideal candidate is a skilled writer with a strong editorial eye, fluency in SEO and GEO best practices and an understanding of how content structure and accuracy shape the way information is surfaced and understood in an AI-driven digital environment. They are equally at home crafting a compelling program page and a three-word call to action, with a keen sense of how language and layout influence the way people read and navigate digital content. Candidates must include a cover letter, along with writing samples or a portfolio link demonstrating range across formats and audiences. This is a hybrid position based on the Brandeis University campus in Waltham, MA, with three days per week in office required. The salary range for the position is $82.0 - 87.2K; Essential & Other Functions Content Creation and Publishing (50%) :  Write compelling, audience-centered content across Brandeis digital properties including academic program pages, marketing landing pages, campaign content and other high-touch areas. Write and edit a range of short-form content — captions, summaries, calls to action, video descriptions and other microcontent that supports a clear user experience (UX). Develop editorial content that showcases Brandeis people, programs and achievements — from quick spotlights to longer-form storytelling as opportunities arise. Adapt and repurpose content across formats while maintaining a consistent institutional voice. Publish and maintain content using a content management system (CMS), ensuring accurate formatting, linking and page structure. Content Strategy & Collaboration (25%) :  Partner with campus stakeholders to translate complex academic and institutional information into clear and effective digital content. Apply SEO and GEO best practices to all content including keyword strategy, metadata, heading structure and linking, with an understanding of how well-structured content performs in AI-driven search environments. Collaborate with designers on visual needs, making strong editorial image selections and optimizing assets for web use. Work with the marketing, web and editorial teams to identify and develop story opportunities aligned with university marketing priorities. Review & Quality (25%) Edit and proofread web content — including content submitted by campus contributors — for clarity, accuracy, tone, accessibility and consistency with Brandeis brand and style guidelines. Conduct content reviews for departmental pages, improving readability, accessibility and messaging as needed. Use web analytics and user behavior data to evaluate content performance and inform improvements. Required Qualifications Bachelor's degree in communications, English, marketing, digital media or a related field. Minimum 3 years of professional experience in web writing or content development. Demonstrated range as a writer — from feature stories to YouTube video descriptions to concise digital microcopy. Working knowledge of SEO principles including metadata, keyword strategy and content structure. Experience with or demonstrated understanding of user-centered content and UX writing principles. Familiarity with web accessibility standards (WCAG) and how they apply to content, including alt text, plain language and heading structure. Experience with a content management system (CMS); familiarity with HTML a plus. Familiarity with AI writing tools and the editorial judgment to use them responsibly in a brand and institutional context. Proficiency in Adobe Photoshop and/or Canva for web image preparation and production. Strong editorial eye and high attention to detail. Ability to manage multiple projects and collaborate effectively across teams. Higher education experience is preferred. Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Area-Coordinator_R0013469-1": {
      "title": "Area Coordinator",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-12\nFull time\n\nJoin our dynamic Community Living team as an Area Coordinator (AC) and lead a vibrant residential community at Brandeis University! In this pivotal role, you will foster a safe, supportive, and engaging environment while overseeing daily operations, supervising Community Advisors (CAs), and driving community development. You’ll work collaboratively with campus partners to promote student engagement, leadership, wellness, and retention while ensuring the effective utilization of residential resources. ACs report directly to an Assistant Director of Community Living.  Primary Responsibilities:  Manage the daily operations of a residential community up to 500 students.  Address student concerns, support roommate conflicts, oversee student staff, coordinating area events, managing facility and public safety issues. Directly supervises 8-15 Community Advisors (CAs), providing training, support, and ongoing performance evaluations.  Serve in a 24/7 emergency response on-call duty rotation, collaborating with campus partners to address urgent resident concerns, critical incidents, and facility emergencies. Serve as a Student Conduct Hearing Officer to adjudicate alleged policy violations while educating students on their Rights and Responsibilities. Complete Hearing Officer training and engage in regular meetings with the Office of Student Rights and Community Standards to uphold fair and consistent practices. Serving on departmental and institutional-wide committees and supporting special projects, including CA Recruitment and Selection, CA Training, and Housing Operations. Actively participate in staff and supervision meetings, as well as department and division-wide professional development training. Salary Range:$48,500–$50,000 This is a live-on position that includes a 2-3 bedroom furnished apartment, partial meal stipend, free on-campus parking, laundry facilities, and access to athletic facilities. For more information about Brandeis University’s comprehensive support for employees’ physical, mental, emotional, and financial health, including medical, dental, and vision care plans, as well as work and life benefits, please visit   Brandeis Employee Benefits . Position Requirements: A bachelor’s degree is required (master’s degree preferred) and 1-3 years of relevant experience. The ideal candidate will possess strong skills in communication, conflict resolution, supervision, problem-solving, time management, and counseling. Proficiency in basic computer skills is expected, and previous experience as a live-on residence life staff member is preferred.   Background Check is required.  Physical Considerations: The Area Coordinator (AC) will occasionally ascend and descend stairs in residential buildings, respond in person to emergency incidents, and move between campus locations as needed. The AC is expected to consistently communicate with others to exchange information while managing daily operations and supporting students. Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/PT-Reserve-Police-Officer_R0013096-1": {
      "title": "PT Reserve Police Officer",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-07-30\nPart time\n\nUnder the direct supervision of the Duty Shift Sergeant, the University Reserve Police Officer will patrol University buildings, grounds, and adjacent areas and enforce the Commonwealth of Massachusetts laws and University regulations therein. Project an image of willing and helpful community service in the interest of campus security, crime prevention, and the overall wellbeing of the general community. University Reserve Police Officers are expected to follow instructions of Public Safety Daily Reports and must be thoroughly familiar with the Brandeis University Campus Police Department Manual. The hourly rate for this position is $35.37 with a minimum of 16 hours per month. Police Officer duties: Patrol University buildings, grounds and adjacent areas at regular intervals. Protect the campus from trespassers, thefts, damage or injury to persons and property. Inspect interior and exterior of all University buildings; extinguish unnecessary light; secure exterior door; lock offices, laboratories and related rooms found unlocked, unless otherwise requested. Report all irregularities to the appropriate authorities. Respond immediately to all intrusion/fire alarms; investigate causes for same and perform necessary related follow-up responsibilities dictated by circumstances at scene and as outlined in written fire alarm response procedures. Exercise police powers in the detection and apprehension of law violators in a judicious manner, exercising discretion and good judgment at all times; investigate all complaints and refer to the appropriate supervisory personnel as deemed necessary. Prepare detailed, clearly written investigative reports of all incidents taking place during tour of duty. Enforce University vehicle parking and traffic regulations. Investigate vehicular accidents and provide required documentation. Control vehicular and pedestrian traffic entering/exiting campus, so as to promote maximum safety. Exercise control over limited parking facilities and exclude unauthorized and undesirable parties from campus properties. May perform overtime campus police detail duties as requested during University social/athletic functions.  May provide emergency and/or courtesy transportation for students, faculty and staff. When assigned to dispatch duty, acknowledge all incoming personal visit/telephone reports at the communication center. Handle all inquiries and/or dispatch patrol cars for action. Control UHF (two-way) communications network at the communications center repeater console, linking the Public Safety office and the patrol cruisers. Monitor alarms and CCTV and dispatch patrol cars for appropriate action. Education & Work Experience: High school diploma or equivalent (associate's degree or higher is preferred) 2 years of relevant work experience Licenses and Qualifications: 21 years of age MA driver's license MA class A license to carry firearms Graduate of MA Full-Time Police Recruit Academy or equivalent POST certified Police Officer Eligible for appointment as a Special State Police Officer in accordance with MGL ch. 22 s.63 Candidates will be required to pass a comprehensive background check, psychological examination, and pre-employment medical exam Reserve Police Officers must commit to the following: Attend annual in-service through the MPTC Complete department firearms qualifications Complete any necessary training as directed by the training officers/Chief of Police Complete annual CPR-AED first responder training. Any other training as deemed necessary Stay abreast of policy changes/dissemination through PMAM Regularly review department email Shift coverage: Officers will be expected to be “on-call” to cover shift work, including dispatch, patrol, and detail work. Officers must work at least 16 hours monthly to stay acclimated with the department and the community.  Mandatory Events: Commencement - late May Annual Department Meeting, late August First-Year Move-In, late August Annual Fall Concert, early fall Annual Spring concert, late spring Expected to make themselves available during university crisis as defined by the Chief of Police. Additional Requirements: Physical demands are those in excess for sedentary work; must be able to remain on feet for extended periods of time, stoop, kneel, crouch, lift, carry, push, pull, climb stairs, balance, walk, and run; must be able to access all facilities on foot; must be able to conduct visual surveillance of individuals and surroundings; must be physically able to operate and communicate using a variety of machines and equipment including baton, automobile, office equipment, radio, telephone, etc; work includes considerable exposure to unusual elements, such as severe weather/ temperatures and loud noises; work environment involves moderate exposure to hazards and physical risks to personal safety. Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Plumber-I_R0013397-1": {
      "title": "Plumber I",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-07-29\nFull time\n\nUnder the direction of the Plumbing Supervisor, the Plumber I installs, troubleshoots, performs preventative maintenance, and repairs plumbing equipment and distribution systems involving work of a varied nature with minimum supervision. This work is accomplished throughout the 100 buildings and 235 acres of Brandeis’ Campus.   Must have extensive knowledge of sanitary codes regarding the installation of plumbing systems and associated equipment. Must have a minimum of five years’ experience in the general maintenance field and be able to demonstrate knowledge and understanding of basic maintenance skills.    The hourly rate for this position is $47.29. This is a 40-hour work week. This position is a Local 615, 32BJ Service Employees International Union (SEIU) position.   Key Responsibilities: Functions: Communicate assignments, schedules, and work status updates with supervisors, staff, and campus partners. Assist with work prioritization, coordination, and distribution as directed. Train, orient, and provide guidance to staff as assigned. Serve as a liaison between union staff, supervisors, contractors, and campus departments. Collaborate with contractors and campus personnel as needed. Attend meetings and obtain required permits as necessary. Main Functions: Install, troubleshoot, maintain, and repair plumbing systems, equipment, fixtures, and related mechanical systems. Perform preventative maintenance and repairs on plumbing distribution systems, pumps, valves, pressure regulators, and laboratory equipment. Install and repair piping systems using appropriate tools and techniques. Read and interpret blueprints, drawings, sketches, and work instructions to complete installations and repairs. Perform testing and inspections to ensure systems meet specifications and code requirements. Identify materials, maintain records, complete reports, and update work orders using the department’s computerized work order system. Diagnose and resolve plumbing issues independently with minimal supervision. Support campus operations during emergencies, events, annual shutdowns, opening of school, and commencement. Operate ladders, lifts, scaffolding, and other equipment required to access work areas. Install appliances and equipment as needed. General Functions: Follow OSHA and university safety procedures and maintain a safe work environment. Report building damage, equipment issues, and maintenance needs. Respond to emergency service calls as needed. Maintain accurate logs, time records, and shift reports. Participate in training to maintain and enhance technical knowledge and skills. Establish positive working relationships with supervisors, coworkers, contractors, and campus community members. Adapt to changing priorities and deadlines. Job Qualifications and Requirements: Prolonged periods of standing and walking.  Must be physically able to climb ladders, bend, or crawl into awkward spaces.  Must work on ladders, bucks trucks, scaffolds, and pits.  Manually lift up to 60 pounds. Heavier material or equipment is lifted using other methods.  Work inside and outside all campus buildings and grounds; including on rooftops, in vaults, and outside throughout campus property.  Work will be performed in and outside during all New England seasons.  Drive a University Vehicle.  Education/Work Experience/Licenses: High School Diploma or GED required, vocational or technical training required. Valid and current United States Driver’s License required. Valid Massachusetts Journeyman’s Plumbing License required. Valid Massachusetts Master Plumbing License required. Valid Massachusetts Certification as a Backflow Prevention Device Tester required. OSHA 10 preferred training. 5 years total work-related experience. Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Temporary-Research-Assistant---School-of-Business-and-Economics--2026_R0013392-1": {
      "title": "Temporary Research Assistant - School of Business and Economics  2026",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-07-23\nPart time\n\nA Research Assistant at the School of Business and Economics assists professors with academic research in the areas of business, economics, econometrics, data analytics, and finance. This position will be supervised by various professors at Brandeis University. Brandeis University is an R1 research institution. A Master's degree is desired. The hourly rate for this position is $15.00. This is a 10 week, 20 hours per week position. Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Assistant-Director--Employer-Success_R0013371-1": {
      "title": "Assistant Director, Employer Success",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-07-21\nFull time\n\nCenter for Careers and the Applied Liberal Arts | Brandeis University Hiring Range: $73,400 - $79,700   Shape the Future of Career-Ready Education Are you interested in playing a critical role in the customer success function of the employer engagement team at Brandeis University? We are seeking an Assistant Director, Employer Success. Working alongside colleagues in employer and alumni engagement, this person turns employer and alumni contacts into returning partners who come back to Brandeis because working with us is easy, responsive, and worth their time. When an employer or alum expresses interest in hiring, hosting students, or getting involved you will make sure they are met quickly, guided through the process, and set up for a positive experience. You will also coordinate across Career Services and university partners so each employer's needs reach the right person at the right time, with the larger goal of building lasting partnerships rather than one-time transactions. What You’ll Do Employer Relationship Success (50%) Serve as project manager and relationship steward for employer and alumni engagements, keeping logistics smooth, tracking current, and follow-up consistent, whether owning the relationship directly or supporting a colleague who does; check in after hires are made and keep partners engaged. Help employers navigate the hiring process from start to finish, including drafting job and internship descriptions; proactively introduce the full range of engagement options so single conversations grow into lasting partnerships Know the Brandeis student value proposition well enough to articulate it confidently, including academics, international student talent, and the depth liberal arts graduates bring Develop working knowledge of internship compliance and regulations around hiring Brandeis students, including international students   Platform and Opportunity Management (35%) Own Handshake from the employer side: help partners create profiles, post jobs and internships, and set up recruiting schedules; monitor active postings, manage applicant bundles, and re-engage employers at the right points in the recruiting calendar; serve as the team's go-to for any employer navigating the platform Use Handshake data to track student interest by industry and role, support contact identification, and inform team outreach strategy Keep CRM records current; coordinate across Career Services to ensure employers get timely access to applicants Build and maintain employer-facing resources, including quick guides to working with Brandeis, job and internship FAQs, and hiring materials that make the process easy for first-time partners   Professional Development and Operations (15%) Participate in professional development activities relevant to employer relations and career services, including regional associations and conferences Share insights from employer interactions with the broader team to inform strategy and outreach Perform other duties as assigned   What We’re Looking For Bachelor's degree required 3-5 years’ experience required Experience in employer relations, career services, customer success, or a related field; backgrounds in account management, client services, or recruiting coordination also welcome Demonstrated ability to manage multiple concurrent relationships and keep processes moving forward with consistent follow-through Strong organizational skills, including consistent use of CRM tools and tracking systems; familiarity with Handshake or similar career management platforms a plus Ability to coordinate across teams and functions to drive outcomes without having direct authority over every participant Clear, professional communicator in writing, by phone and in person Familiarity with internship compliance and regulations around hiring international students is helpful Why Brandeis? Brandeis University is making a significant investment in experiential learning as part of its vision for the future of liberal arts education. Students are already participating in exceptional internships, research, community-engaged learning, and applied projects across the university. Our next step is building a coordinated, university-wide ecosystem that expands access to high-quality experiential learning, strengthens partnerships across the university, and makes these opportunities a defined and recognized part of every Brandeis student's education. To Apply: Please submit a cover letter with your application for consideration. You should upload the letter with your resume; there is not a separate prompt for a cover letter in the candidate application system. Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Senior-Associate-Director-of-Experiential-Learning_R0013366-1": {
      "title": "Senior Associate Director of Experiential Learning",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-07-21\nFull time\n\nCenter for Careers and the Applied Liberal Arts  |  Brandeis University Hiring Range: $99,300 - $116,000 We’re seeking a Senior Associate Director of Experiential Learning to help lead that transformation. This newly created role offers the rare opportunity to shape a university-wide initiative from the ground up. You’ll establish standards, create scalable processes, develop strategic partnerships, and help define how experiential learning is delivered across the university. If you’re excited by building and growing something with strong institutional support, long-term visibility, and meaningful impact on students, this is the role for you. About the Role You’ll lead the development and operation of experiential learning at Brandeis, including internships, applied research partnerships, field-based projects, shadowing, fellowships, and community-engaged work, serving undergraduate and graduate students across all five schools. Working closely with employers, alumni, faculty, donors, IT, and campus partners, you’ll develop partnerships, create  systems and processes, and help connect students with meaningful opportunities outside  the classroom. You’ll also help shape how high-impact experiences are recognized alongside students’ academic achievements, contributing to a university-wide framework that documents learning from the classroom to the street. . This role is ideal for someone who enjoys building strong relationships, collaborating across a complex organization, and turning ambitious ideas into practical, sustainable programs. What You’ll Do Program Development and External Partnerships (50%) Build and grow Brandeis’s portfolio of experiential learning opportunities across all five schools, including internships, research partnerships, fellowships, and community-engaged work. Set the standards for what qualifies as an endorsed experience, including for the university’s new second transcript. Partner with Career Programs and Employer Relations to develop employer-led projects, alumni-connected experiences, and gift-funded initiatives. Operations, Systems, and Student Access (30%) Design  and maintain a student platform for experiential learning opportunities, in partnership with University IT. Keep the opportunity catalog accurate and easy for students and advisors to navigate. Standardize how the university documents high impact experiences, and work with student-facing teams on the resources students need to find and use them. Assessment and Data (10%) Build and maintain a framework for tracking participation, program quality, and outcomes, in partnership with the Center’s data team. Use that data to evaluate impact and contribute to university-level reporting on experiential learning. Team Leadership (10%) Supervise and develop one assistant director. Represent the experiential learning function across the university, working with faculty, IT, career counseling staff, deans, and senior leadership. What You Bring Bachelor’s degree required, Master’s preferred. Five to eight years of progressively responsible experience in experiential learning, internship programs, career services, or a closely related field. A track record of building programs and processes. Strong relationship-building skills across employers, faculty, donors, students, IT colleagues, and senior university leadership, with a record of turning those relationships into active, sustained partnerships. Sound operational judgment and the ability to move work forward in a complex, multi-stakeholder environment. At least one year of supervisory experience managing professional staff. Clear, direct communication skills, in writing and in person, with the presence to represent the Center credibly across a wide range of audiences. Nice to Have Master’s degree in higher education administration, student affairs, counseling, or a related field. Experience navigating a complex, decentralized, multi-school university environment. Experience with Handshake, Salesforce, Workday, or similar platforms. Working knowledge of CPT/OPT and international student work authorization. Familiarity with experiential learning credentialing frameworks, co-curricular records, or similar models for documenting student experiences. Experience working with donors or development colleagues on endowed programs, named fellowships, or gift-funded initiatives. Why Brandeis? Brandeis University is making a significant investment in experiential learning as part of its vision for the future of liberal arts education. Students are already participating in exceptional internships, research, community-engaged learning, and applied projects across the university. Our next step is building a coordinated, university-wide ecosystem that expands access to high-quality experiential learning, strengthens partnerships across the university, and makes these opportunities a defined and recognized part of every Brandeis student's education. To Apply:  Please submit a cover letter with your application for consideration. You should upload the letter with your resume; there is not a separate prompt for a cover letter in the candidate application system. Reports to: Executive Director of External Engagement Direct reports: One assistant director Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Temporary-Equipment-Mechanic-I--Lead_R0013149-1": {
      "title": "Temporary Equipment Mechanic I, Lead",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-07-17\nFull time\n\nBrandeis University is delighted to announce a new opportunity as the Temporary Equipment Mechanic I, Lead in the Facilities Department. Under the direction of the Manager, Grounds & Fleet, the Equipment Mechanic I, Lead, maintains, troubleshoots, diagnoses and repairs all types of vehicles and equipment owned and leased by Brandeis University. Manages the Facilities Services Fleet inspection program. Must have an extensive knowledge of diagnosing, repairing and maintaining all small engines as well as gas and diesel vehicles and equipment. This position is a Local 615, 32BJ Service Employees International Union (SEIU) position. The hourly rate for this position is $43.47, 40 hours per week. This position is estimated to be 3 months in duration. Job Functions: Lead Equipment Mechanic Functions: Must have sufficient knowledge of small engines, gas and diesel engines, hydraulics and electrical systems. Relay information and assignments to others as directed by manager. Instruct and direct fellow employees assigned to assist Lead equipment mechanic Schedule routine and unexpected maintenance on all Brandeis owned/leased vehicles and equipment Schedules and ensures required Brandeis and State inspections are up to date Maintain parts and supply inventory for maintenance of Brandeis owned and leased vehicles and equipment. Assess and update all work order assignments using the department's computerized work order management system. This includes keeping records, writing reports, and identifying/requesting materials, and recording time in a timely manner. Make recommendations pertaining to the condition and longevity of all University owned/leased vehicles and equipment Main Functions: Diagnose nature and extent of equipment and vehicle malfunction. Raise vehicles and equipment utilizing hydraulic and floor jacks to gain access Replace, adjust, install and repair accessories (radios, heaters, mirrors, windshield wipers, headlights etc.) Utilize Equipment requiring a MA hoisting license Lubricate equipment and change engine and hydraulic oil and transmission fluids. Repair and install tires using a tire changing machine Corrects vehicle deficiencies by removing, repairing, adjusting, overhauling, assembling, disassembling, and replacing major assemblies, sub-assemblies, components, parts, or systems, such as, power and drive trains, electrical, air conditioning, fuel, emission, brake, steering, and hydraulics Utilize trucks and utility vehicles with plows and sanders, as well as front end loaders to remove and treat snow and ice from all areas on campus. Maintains and repairs small engine equipment including blowers, string trimmers and lawn mowers including blade sharpening Assist the Groundspersons team as directed by the manager. General Functions: Follow proper safety procedures and observe all safety rules and regulations including OSHA Respond to emergency call-ins as needed Read, write, understand, and carry out oral and written instructions in English. Participates in training to keep and acquire knowledge, skills, and abilities in order to remain current with standard operational practices and revised standards Establish and maintain a positive cooperative working relationship with supervisors, fellow workers, department heads, and other members of the campus community. Work in an environment subject to frequent changing climate conditions, priorities and deadlines. Drive a University Vehicle requiring Class B Commercial Driver’s License *This list is representative of those duties and responsibilities that are required of this position. However, the list is not to be considered as all-inclusive. Education, Licenses and Work Experience: Education: High School Diploma or GED required, Mechanic training required. Valid and current United States Driver’s License required. Massachusetts Hoisting License 1C/2A required. DOT Medical Certificate. Work Experience: 5 years of practical experience on repairing and maintaining small engine, gas, and diesel vehicles and equipment required. 1 year of supervisory experience in shop/garage preferred. Job Qualifications and Requirements: Physical/Environmental Demands: Prolonged periods of standing and walking. Work may be performed outside during all New England seasons. Manually lift up to 60 pounds. Heavier material or equipment is lifted using other methods. Drive a University Vehicle. Additional Requirements/Qualifications: Frequent use of verbal and written skills through the work order system. May explain and offer guidance on routine procedures. Must have an extensive understanding of gas and diesel engines. Must be able to use OBD II diagnostic computer Must be able to use hand, power and pneumatic tools and lifts Has frequent contact with other employees, professional staff and/or external contacts to give or obtain information or deliver service. Results can affect our own functional area. Must have the ability to follow instructions from supervisors or senior managers. Must be able to direct and work with other staff members. Decisions may affect a work unit or area within a department. May contribute to business and operational decisions that affect the department.   Problems are varied, requiring analysis or interpretation of the situation. Problems are solved using knowledge and skills, general precedents, and practices. Results are defined. Determines how to accomplish results with little guidance. Supervision/manager provides broad guidance and overall direction. Must be able to work within Facilities Services budget. Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Custodian_R0013042-1": {
      "title": "Custodian",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-07-09\nFull time\n\nBrandeis University is looking for a Custodian in Facilities Department. Essential Job Functions: Clean, strip, polish building surfaces as directed. Read, understand and carry out simple instructions in English. Establish and maintain a positive working relationship with supervisors, fellow workers, department heads and other members of the campus community. Follow simple oral instructions in English. Work in an environment subject to frequently changing priorities and from individuals and time deadlines. Bend, stoop, lift and move furniture, shovel snow and remove trash weighing 30 pounds. Work either independently or in a project team setting. Organize responsibilities and tasks. Receive and ship packages, luggage and supplies. Set up rooms for conferences, lectures, and special events. Respond to and clean up after emergencies and disasters. Assist in the security and protection of facilities to include locking and unlocking exterior and interior doors. *This list is representative of those duties and responsibilities that are required of this position. However, the list is not to be considered as all inclusive. Schedule: Wednesday - Sunday; 3:00 PM - 11:00 PM Hourly Rate: $26.95 + $1.70 shift differential, this is a 40-hour work week Job Qualifications and Requirements: Ability to clean, strip, polish building surfaces as directed. Ability to read and understand simple instructions in English. Ability to follow simple oral instructions in English. Ability to establish and maintain a positive working relationship with supervisors, fellow workers, department heads and other members of the campus community. Ability to work effectively in an environment subject to frequently changing priorities and periodic stress from individuals and deadlines. Ability to bend, stoop, lift and move furniture, shovel snow and remove trash weighing 30 pounds. Ability to work effectively and efficiently either independently or in a project team setting. Ability to organize responsibilities and tasks weekly to perform them efficiently and effectively. Ability to respond to and clean up after emergencies and disasters. Ability to perform other related tasks as required. Job Location (Place(s) where work is performed): Inside campus buildings and outside perimeter of buildings. Equipment (Machines, devices, tools, etc., used in job performance): Floor buffers, vacuums of all types, wet/vacuums, automatic walk-behind and riding floor scrubbers, pressure washers, 44 gallon trash containers, snow shovels, ice chippers, brooms, mops, pails with ringers, chemicals including but not limited to: ammonia, bleach, germicide/disinfectant, floor stripper, wax, ice melt, and metal polish.   Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Temporary-Research-Assistant_R0013261-1": {
      "title": "Temporary Research Assistant",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-06-04\nPart time\n\nThe Institute for Behavioral Health seeks an Undergraduate Research Assistant (URA) as summer intern to provide data collection and general support the Exploring Community Knowledge -Ongoing (ECKS-O) study. This is a part time internship, 20 hour per week from June - August 2026. The pay rate is $18/hour. Essential Duties and Responsibilities:   The Undergraduate Research Assistant summer intern will work as part of a team to: Working with Project Managers, to execute project administrative tasks. Assist with data collection efforts for survey data for the ECKS-O study. This will be done in the field and remotely with research participants. Work alongside research staff to support quality control activities (e.g., quantitative data, qualitative data, proofreading transcriptions, etc.) Support quantitative and qualitative data analysis.   Contribute to statistical analysis (e.g. bivariate analysis) and/or qualitative data coding with ECKS-O data. Experience with SPSS, R or Stata is preferred. Conduct qualitative analysis, experience with Dedoose preferred. Assist in creation/preparation of presentations, documentation, and other communication materials to delivery of information to communities, the department of public health, the team, and more Attend weekly project meetings to record action steps, as requested. Skills and Experience Requirements: The candidate must have strong organizational and time-management skills, must be detailed oriented and focused. Must have ability to treat people who use drugs and people who are in recovery with dignity, respect, and kindness. Excellent communication skills, both written and verbal are necessary. Training and mentoring available for candidate. Prior experience with research, community service with vulnerable populations, and/or with people who use drugs is preferred. Access to transportation is required. Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Adjunct-Instructor-in-Technology-Innovation--Product-Development--and-Strategic-Leadership_R0013230": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor in Technology Innovation, Product Development, and Strategic Leadership",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-05-24\nPart time\n\nBrandeis University’s Online Information Technology Management Program is seeking an Adjunct Faculty member for  RITM 135 Technology Innovation, Product Development, and Strategic Leadership for the Fall-2 2026 session. This 3-credit asynchronous online course is an 8-week requirement for the Master of Science in Information Technology Management. This course explores innovation lifecycles, product design principles, strategic decision-making, and leadership in technology organizations. Students learn to guide innovation initiatives from concept through execution, balancing technical feasibility with organizational impact. Core Course Responsibilities Summary Course Logistics and Facilitation : Focuses on the organized and timely rollout of course content, maintaining consistent communication through weekly announcements, and ensuring all instructional activities occur within university-approved digital platforms. Instructor Presence and Engagement : Centers on building an active teaching persona by hosting live introductory sessions, facilitating weekly academic discourse in forums, and maintaining regular availability for student consultation. Individual Feedback and Grading : Emphasizes the professional obligation to provide transparent, rubric-based evaluations and supportive commentary on student work within a standardized weekly timeframe. Professional Conduct and Standards : Requires adherence to university communication protocols, the promotion of respectful online \"netiquette,\" and ensuring the course meets accessibility and technical visibility standards before and during the term. Qualifications: Required: Master’s degree in Technology Management, Innovation Management, Business Administration (technology focus), Information Systems, Engineering Management, or a closely related field Minimum 3 years of professional experience in technology innovation, product development, digital transformation, or strategic technology leadership Demonstrated experience guiding products or innovation initiatives from concept through launch and lifecycle management Experience with product strategy, roadmap development, stakeholder alignment, and cross-functional leadership Minimum 1 year experience developing asynchronous online courses for adult learners in higher education At least 1 year of teaching or training experience (preferably online/asynchronous) Experience with online instruction Excellent communication and teaching skills in an online learning environment. Preferred: Doctorate (PhD or DBA) in Technology Management, Innovation, Business Strategy, Information Systems, Organizational Leadership, or related field 10+ years of experience in senior technology leadership, product management, innovation strategy, or executive-level decision-making roles Professional experience as a Chief Technology Officer (CTO), Chief Product Officer (CPO), VP of Product, Innovation Director, or similar role Experience with innovation frameworks (e.g., Lean Startup, Design Thinking, Stage-Gate, Agile product development) Industry certifications such as PMP, SAFe Agilist, Certified Scrum Product Owner (CSPO), or equivalentExperience teaching or developing graduate-level online courses Experience teaching or developing graduate-level online courses Prior online teaching experience at the graduate level  Knowledge of global learner personas and culturally responsive pedagogy Familiarity with Moodle LMS and digital authoring tools (e.g., H5P) Interested candidates should submit: A cover letter highlighting relevant qualifications and teaching experience. A current CV or resume. Contact information for three professional references. Application review begins June 1, 2026 though we will continue to accept submissions on an ongoing basis. This appointment is to a position that is in a collective bargaining unit represented by SEIU Local 509. Compensation for this positon is: $6573.15 Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
