    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Assistant-Professor-of-Biochemistry_R0013081": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Biochemistry",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-04-15\nFull time\n\nThe Department of Biochemistry at Brandeis University invites applications for a full-time Assistant Professor position to begin July 1, 2026. The position carries a two-year appointment with the possibility of renewal and is outside the tenure structure. Teaching responsibilities will span the Biochemistry and Biology/HSSP programs and include an introductory and advanced introductory biochemistry class (BCHM 88 and BCHM 100), a project lab in protein biochemistry (BIOL 251), an elective course targeting Biochemistry majors (e.g., BIBC 126 Molecular Mechanisms of Disease), and an elective course appropriate for non-majors (e.g., CDSC 7 Culinary Chemistry), totaling five courses per year. Requirements for the position include a Ph.D. in biochemistry, or a related field, and a strong commitment to teaching and mentoring students at the undergraduate level. Interested applicants should submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, and statement of teaching philosophy. In addition, applicants should arrange for three letters of recommendation to be submitted on their behalf to biochemistry@brandeis.edu . First consideration will be given to applications received by May 15, 2026. Questions about the position may be directed to Dan Oprian ( oprian@brandeis.edu) . The salary range for this position is $67, 000 to $85,000, c ommensurate with experience. Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/HVAC-III_R0012338": {
      "title": "HVAC III",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-04-09\nFull time\n\nUnder the direction of the HVAC Supervisor, the HVAC III Technician maintains and repairs heating, ventilation and cooling systems and refrigeration systems throughout campus. Work includes building temperature control systems and preventative maintenance. Will also be responsible for completing assigned work orders relating to general HVAC duties. The hourly rate for this position is $36.50. Key Responsibilities: Electrical Related: 85% - Install, maintain, and repair a variety of HVAC and refrigeration equipment (e.g. centrifugal, compressors, steam absorption units, air compressors, fan coil units, fan units, pumps, cooling towers and related piping.) Will work with and up to 10 tons of refrigeration. Locates and diagnose trouble in entire HVAC and Refrigeration Systems and Equipment, to include diagnosing electrical and mechanical faults for HVAC systems. Maintains pneumatic and electric controls systems associated with refrigeration equipment Determine materials to be used and quantities needed. Utilizes a variety of tools and instruments associated with the trade. Must observe safety regulations at all times. Cleans work area and maintains tools and equipment Performs similar and related work as required. Receives and completes general work orders that may not be HVAC related Provides appropriate support during events, annual electrical shutdown, opening of school and commencement General: 15% - Follows proper safety procedures and observes all safety rules and regulations including OSHA Reports building damages, malfunctioning equipment, needed maintenance and/or repairs in assigned work areas  Responds to emergency call ins  Inputs data into computerized work order system, recording time and work order information Participates in training to keep and acquire knowledge, skills and abilities in order to remain current with standard operational practices and revised standards  Maintains appropriate certifications and licenses Interacts professionally and follows up with members of the campus community Continue to actively pursue time/education hours towards MA license until achieved. Physical/Environmental Demands Must be physically able to perform all of the essential duties of the position as set forth in this Position Description, including but not limited to the following: Observe safety rules at all times Read, write, understand and carry out oral and written instructions in English Work on ladders and scaffolds, and in bucket trucks, pits and manholes Utilize a variety of HVAC tools and instruments Drive a University vehicle Manually lift up to 60 pounds (heavier material or equipment is lifted using other methods) Job Requirements: Education: HS diploma or GED with vocational or technical training required; associate's degree preferred Experience: 2 years of documented work experience maintaining and installing HVAC systems Licenses and Certifications: OHSA-10 Certification Required, EPA Certification/License to handle refrigerant Required, HVAC License issued by the Commonwealth of Massachusetts Preferred; actively pursuing HVAC License issued by the Commonwealth of Massachusetts with the intent of earning it if not yet attained. Valid and current Massachusetts Driver’s License - Required Basic knowledge of electrical or plumbing equipment in order to assist those trades in making minor repairs and adjustments. Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Adjunct-Professor-in-Strategy-and-Innovation_R0012989": {
      "title": "Adjunct Professor in Strategy and Innovation",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-03-25\nPart time\n\nThe School of Business and Economics (SBE) at Brandeis University seeks instructors to teach a four credit course in Strategy and Innovation in AY 26/27. These courses are part of the graduate curriculum (MBA and specialized master’s programs) and may include topics such as: Competition and Strategy (core strategy course) Alliances and Mergers & Acquisitions Strategy Innovation Strategy Technology Management Platform Strategy and Digital Ecosystems Corporate Strategy and Growth Courses are designed to provide students with rigorous frameworks and practical tools to analyze competitive environments, formulate strategy, and understand how firms create and sustain competitive advantage in dynamic markets. All courses are taught using case-based and/or experiential learning methods (including case discussions, simulations, and applied projects), and emphasize active student participation. Course assignments and scheduling will depend on program needs and instructor expertise. Candidates should have a minimum qualification of a Master’s degree (PhD preferred) in strategy, management, economics, sociology, or a related field, along with relevant professional and/or teaching experience. We particularly welcome applicants with industry experience in consulting, technology, innovation management, corporate strategy, entrepreneurship, or related fields. Experience in teaching using case-based or experiential methodologies is strongly preferred. Interested individuals should submit a cover letter, CV, course syllabi, a brief teaching statement, and names and contact information for two references. The deadline for first consideration is April 15, 2026. This appointment is to a position that is in a collective bargaining unit represented by SEIU Local 509. Compensation for this position is $10,000/four-credit course. Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Temporary-Microcredential-Course-Developer--Applied-Problem-Solving-Techniques-for-STEM-Teams_R0012210-1": {
      "title": "Temporary Microcredential Course Developer: Applied Problem-Solving Techniques for STEM Teams",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-02-12\nPart time\n\nHelp STEM teams solve real-world problems—one framework at a time. Brandeis University’s Rabb School of Continuing Studies is seeking an experienced academic to design and build a 10–15 hour online asynchronous micro credential course leading to a microcredential in Applied Problem-Solving Techniques for STEM Teams . In this fully remote, short-term contract, you’ll create a dynamic, industry-relevant training program that equips professionals with practical methods to analyze, evaluate, and resolve complex challenges in cross-disciplinary STEM environments. Learners will gain mastery of frameworks, collaboration tools, and outcome-driven techniques they can apply immediately in technical workplaces. What You Will Do Develop structured, interactive content (10–15 hours) for self-paced online learning. Design assessments and rubrics to measure applied problem-solving proficiency. Build engaging materials including simulations, real-world case studies, and multimedia. Incorporate WCAG accessibility standards for inclusive learning. Collaborate with instructional designers to optimize delivery, interactivity, and clarity. Recommend tools, readings, and resources aligned with current STEM team practices. Use technology-enhanced learning elements—animations, avatars, adaptive platforms, gamification—where appropriate. What You Bring: Master’s degree (Doctorate preferred) in engineering, mathematics, science, instructional design, or related field. 2+ years of relevant professional experience in STEM collaboration, innovation, or project-based problem solving. At least 1 year of teaching or training experience (preferably online/asynchronous). Comfort with LMS platforms and digital authoring tools. Familiarity with analytical tools, collaborative platforms, and interdisciplinary teamwork. Strong communication, organization, and independent work skills. Preferred: Experience designing online training programs or micro-credentials. Familiarity with Moodle LMS. Background in adaptive learning or simulation-based instruction. Details: Fully remote (U.S.-based applicants only, no visa sponsorships) 6-week development timeline (~25 total hours) Compensation: $1,000 Ready to help STEM professionals tackle challenges with precision and creativity? Apply today and join Brandeis University in shaping the next generation of technical problem solvers. Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Temporary-Exhibition-Installer_R0012617": {
      "title": "Temporary Exhibition Installer",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-01-05\nPart time\n\nDescription Under the supervision and direction of the Manager, Collections and Exhibitions, the Assistant Preparator provides support for all aspects of exhibition production including: installation, preparation of artwork, fine art packing and unpacking, general construction, wall preparation, gallery maintenance (lighting and cleaning), equipment/supply inventories or other duties as assigned. Hourly Payrate: $27-$30 Essential Responsibilities Professional art handling of works of art (2D, 3D, multimedia) including both collection and loan objects. Knowledge of safe and museum standard installation practices for both special temporary exhibitions, gallery rotations or longer-term collections installation. Experience with fine art packing, unpacking and movement of artwork within museum standards and under direction. Installation of exhibition graphics, labels or other materials. Basic Qualifications   Experience with art handling, installation methodology within museum industry standards. Deadline-driven, responds creatively to situations, problem-solving, and ability to carry out instructions precisely and collaboratively in a team environment. Basic knowledge and familiarity with power / hand tools and hardware. Physical Demands Must be able to lift up to 40lbs. Must be able to stand, walk, carry, stoop, kneel, twist, squat bend and reach overhead. Comfortable with heights, climbing ladders and mechanical lifts. Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Temp-Course-Assistant-Grader_R0012569": {
      "title": "Temp Course Assistant/Grader",
      "description": "Posted: 2025-12-18\nPart time\n\nSeveral Course Assistant positions are available for non student for Graduate Courses. Course Assistant duties may include but are not limited to assisting the professor with: course preparation before and during the semester; Latte maintenance; grading essays exams and homework; syllabus design; writing exam questions. Course Assistants may be required to attend classes on a regular basis. Course Assistants may also be responsible for some instructional duties including the following: lead discussion sessions, science labs, and recitations and/or teach an occasional class. They may also be responsible for some student advising and may be required to hold office hours. Qualifications: Course Assistants must have successfully completed graduate-level coursework in the area to which he or she is assigned. Must have achieved high grades in the same class they are assisting in (or an equivalent course as determined by instructor or professor). Excellent interpersonal, writing, and communication skills. Must demonstrate a high level of critical thinking and analysis. Must demonstrate attention to detail and strong time-management capabilities.   Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Per-Diem-Provider_R0010950-2": {
      "title": "Per Diem Provider",
      "description": "Posted: 2025-11-26\nPart time\n\nSupporting the Brandeis Mission the  Per diem Provider(s) provides direct care to students within their scope of practice in an ambulatory care setting relative to physical diagnosis and therapeutic management. The per diem provider may act as a clinical resource to Health Center staff and works closely with the multi-specialty team to develop and coordinate a plan of care to meet the needs of our students. This is a temporary position. Hiring range for this position if filled as an NP is $68/hr. - $73/hr. and if filled by an MD is $99/hr. - $112/hr. Job Duties: Provide culturally and clinically competent direct patient care for students of Brandeis University within board certified scope of practice and elicits history, performs physical examinations, orders and interprets laboratory and diagnostic tests, determines diagnosis(es), and develops plan of care with education to patients and their families as appropriate. Responsible for assuring that documentation meets qualitative and billing standards. Coordinates the care of patients in order to provide safer, effective, responsive, and timely student centered care. Provides additional education and outreach for students as identified. Promotes multiculturalism and inclusivity for all students. Strong analytical and problem-solving skills with the ability to provide individualized care to patients. Ability to maintain effective records and documentation of patient care.  May oversee immunization compliance services for the RN. Is a clinical resource to support staff. Job Requirements and Additional Information: Must meet requirements for level hired: Master's degree in Nursing. Active Registered Nurse/NP License for practice through MA Board of Nursing - Re-Current Board Certification in NP specialty area; Active Medical Doctor license. Current Basic Life Support. Active unencumbered DEA and MCSR Required Excellent communication and interpersonal skills to collaborate with other healthcare professionals and educate patients and their families. Requires exerting up to 20 pounds of force frequently. Job is physical in nature and employee needs to stand and move throughout shift. Provides face-to-face, hands on care. Uses universal precautions, infection control practices, and environmental precautions in line with industry standards to reduce work related exposures. Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Temporary-Community-Educator_R0012511-1": {
      "title": "Temporary Community Educator",
      "description": "Posted: 2025-11-25\nFull time\n\nBetter to Learn is a partnership between Brandeis University and Legacy Heritage Fund that brings together Jewish teens in weekly online learning sessions. In addition, students are invited to the Boston area for a four-day residency in late December to engage in thoughtful learning, hear guest speakers, connect with their online peers and visit Brandeis University. We seek Community Educators to support students and programming at the Better to Learn December Retreat. Responsibilities Attend the December 19-22 Retreat and stay in the hotel ( Hilton Garden Inn, Waltham ) with students. Supervise Better to Learn students at the hotel, on the program bus and on campus during all activities, including in the evening after programming has ended (ie: make sure students are in their rooms, at breakfast and on the bus in the morning; handle any student concerns or disciplinary issues; address minor medical issues–with support from the Residential Coordinator). In collaboration with other Community Educators, run 7 programs (already created-–timing ranges from 30 minutes to 1 hour 45 mins) connected to the program theme. Provide administrative support to Better to Learn Directors, Instructors and Guest Speakers as requested (ie: get supplies and materials, make copies, set up and break down program spaces, including meals). Act as a positive Jewish role model for Better to Learn participants. Qualifications Current undergraduate, recent college graduate or graduate student. Experience as a camp counselor, youth worker or religious schoolteacher. Experience working with and supervising teens. Creative, flexible and enthusiastic. Compensation : $800 plus room and board (kosher catering for all meals provided by Dushez Catering ). Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Adjunct-Instructor-in-Sustainable-Biotechnology_R0011697": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor in Sustainable Biotechnology",
      "description": "Posted: 2025-11-06\nPart time\n\nBrandeis University's Graduate Professional Studies (GPS) is looking for an industry leader to teach in our Master of Science in Applied Biotechnology & Enterprise program. Brandeis University is consistently ranked among the nation's top universities, and our online courses are developed using best practices in online learning. Information about Brandeis University and Graduate Professional Studies can be found online. Course Title: Sustainable Biotechnology This course examines the development and implementation of environmentally sustainable technologies in biotechnology. Topics include renewable bioproducts, green bioprocessing, circular bioeconomy models, and environmental regulatory compliance. Students will explore how sustainable practices are applied across the biotech industry, including pharmaceuticals, agriculture, and energy. Qualifications Qualified candidates will have Subject Matter Qualifications in the following areas. Required: - Current active employment in the relevant biotech or pharmaceutical industry - Deep knowledge of course-specific applications and industry practices - Ph.D. or M.S. - Strong communication and analytical skills Preferred: - Previous teaching experience or experience delivering professional training - Familiarity with online instructional tools and platforms General Responsibilities - Design a syllabus following program chair guidance and the syllabus template - Create content aligned with course outcomes, integrating personal expertise - Facilitate online discussions and provide timely, relevant feedback - Maintain the course site and update content in the Moodle LMS - Introduce frameworks for life cycle assessment (LCA) and sustainability metrics. - Guide students in evaluating case studies of green innovation in industrial biotechnology. General Skill Requirements - Strong interpersonal skills and the ability to foster student engagement - Written communication skills, especially for online learning - Ability to manage time effectively and respond promptly to students - Familiarity with sustainable development goals and environmental impact analysis tools. About the Master of Science in Applied Biotechnology & Enterprise This program prepares students to innovate and lead in the fast-paced biotech industry, integrating scientific knowledge with business strategy. Students learn through project-based, real-world applications. All GPS courses are 8-weeks long and taught asynchronously online. About GPS Faculty GPS Faculty are active industry professionals who teach part-time, online. Our instructors hold at least a master's degree, and many have terminal degrees and professional certifications. Previous teaching experience is not required; GPS provides full training. Application Process: Interested candidates should submit: A cover letter highlighting relevant qualifications and teaching experience. A current CV or resume. Contact information for three professional references. (Optional) Examples of teaching materials or professional publications related to leadership and industry innovation. This appointment is to a position that is in a collective bargaining unit represented by SEIU Local 509.  The compensation for this position is $6241.20. Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Temporary-Theatrical-Electrician_R0012432": {
      "title": "Temporary Theatrical Electrician",
      "description": "Posted: 2025-11-05\nPart time\n\nWe are seeking skilled and reliable Temporary Overhire Electricians to supplement our core lighting staff during peak production periods. This role is project-based and primarily focused on the physical execution of lighting load-ins, focus calls, and strikes. The ideal candidate can work efficiently as part of a team, follow directions precisely, and adhere to all safety standards. The hourly rate for the position is $22.50 Key Responsibilities Load-in: Read lighting plots to hang, circuit, cable, and patch lighting instruments and practicals. Focus: Assist the Lighting Designer and Master Electrician during focus sessions, including physically positioning instruments from ladders, catwalks, or personnel lifts. Strike: Assist in the safe and efficient dismantling, sorting, and storage of all lighting equipment at the end of a production. Equipment Prep: Perform basic equipment checks and preparation as directed by the Master Electrician. Safety: Strictly adhere to all safety protocols, including the proper use of personal protective equipment (PPE) and safe working practices at heights. Collaboration: Work collaboratively with other production departments to ensure a smooth and safe workflow. Required Skills and Qualifications Proven experience as a stage electrician in a professional or academic setting. Strong working knowledge of theatrical lighting equipment, including conventional and LED fixtures. Understanding of basic electrical principles, power distribution, and safe working practices. Familiarity with DMX and other signal protocols. Ability to read and interpret lighting plots and hookup charts. Must be comfortable working at heights, including on ladders, catwalks, and aerial work platforms. Ability to work efficiently as part of a team in a fast-paced environment. Must be reliable, punctual, and have a positive, solutions-oriented attitude. Preferred Qualifications Proficiency in operating ETC Eos family lighting consoles. Physical Requirements & Work Environment Ability to lift, push, or pull objects up to 50 lbs. Must be able to climb, balance, stoop, kneel, and crouch. Ability to stand and work for extended periods. Work environment includes exposure to heights, enclosed spaces, dust, and moderate noise levels. Hours are project-dependent and will include evenings and weekends. Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Temporary-Micro-Credential-Grader---Industry-Focused-Prompt-Engineering-for-ROI-Driven-Results_R0012425-2": {
      "title": "Temporary Micro-Credential Grader – Industry-Focused Prompt Engineering for ROI-Driven Results",
      "description": "Posted: 2025-11-04\nPart time\n\nLocation: Fully remote (U.S.-based applicants only, no visa sponsorships) Division: Rabb School of Continuing Studies, Brandeis University Type: Part-Time, 4 months, varying hours,  no more than 25 hours per week Compensation: Hourly $25–$30 Reports to: Assistant Dean of Education and Learning Innovation Brandeis University’s Rabb School of Continuing Studies is seeking a detail-oriented professional with expertise in applied AI to serve as a Micro-Credential Grader for the online asynchronous credential, Industry-Focused Prompt Engineering for ROI-Driven Results. In this fully remote, short-term hourly position, you’ll evaluate learner submissions that demonstrate practical mastery in designing, testing, and refining prompts for large language models (LLMs) to support measurable organizational objectives. This credential equips professionals with the ability to align prompt strategies with business goals, evaluate platform performance, and quantify the ROI impact of AI-driven solutions. As a grader, you’ll apply structured rubrics to assess strategic thinking, prompt engineering fluency, and outcome-based reasoning. This role offers a unique opportunity to contribute to a cutting-edge, workforce-aligned credential that bridges AI innovation with business impact. What You Will Do Evaluate learner submissions that include prompt design portfolios, platform performance analyses, and ROI impact assessments tied to real-world business use cases. Apply structured rubrics to assess mastery of skills such as LLM prompt iteration, use-case alignment, performance benchmarking, and ROI quantification. Review learner reflections on prompt strategy effectiveness, ethical considerations, and organizational integration. Participate in calibration exercises with fellow graders (if needed) to ensure consistency in evaluating strategic, technical, and business-oriented artifacts. Maintain confidentiality and objectivity throughout the grading process. What You Bring Bachelor’s degree required; Master’s degree preferred in Computer Science, Data Science, Business Analytics, or related disciplines. Subject-matter expertise in prompt engineering, LLM capabilities, and AI deployment for business outcomes. Experience in academic assessment, workforce development, or digital learning preferred. Familiarity with learning management systems (Moodle preferred), online credentialing platforms, and collaborative grading workflows. Professional, learner-centered approach with a commitment to academic integrity and continuous improvement. Proficient in rubric-based assessment and competency validation, especially for strategic and project-based submissions. Strong attention to detail and ability to maintain consistency across diverse learner artifacts. Excellent written communication skills for delivering constructive, learner-focused feedback. Comfortable working in asynchronous learning environments and using digital platforms. Adaptability in managing multiple grading tasks within deadlines. Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Temporary-Micro-Credential-Grader---JSD--Microcredential-Grader-AI-Fundamentals-for-STEM-Professionals_R0012424-1": {
      "title": "Temporary Micro-Credential Grader - JSD- Microcredential Grader-AI Fundamentals for STEM Professionals",
      "description": "Posted: 2025-11-04\nPart time\n\nPosition: Micro-Credential Grader-AI Fundamentals for STEM Professionals Location: Fully remote (U.S.-based applicants only, no visa sponsorships) Division: Rabb School of Continuing Studies, Brandeis University Type: Part-Time, 4 months, varying hours, no more than 25 hours per week Compensation: Hourly $25-$30 Reports to: Assistant Dean of Education and Learning Innovation Brandeis University’s Rabb School of Continuing Studies is seeking a detail-oriented STEM professional to serve as a Micro-Credential Grader for the online asynchronous credential, AI Fundamentals for STEM Professionals. In this fully remote, short-term hourly position, you’ll evaluate learner submissions that demonstrate mastery of AI concepts through a real-world STEM challenge and a complete 5-step workflow design. This project-based credential equips professionals with foundational skills in supervised learning, data preprocessing, model selection, and ethical AI deployment. As a grader, you’ll apply structured rubrics to assess technical accuracy, conceptual depth, and responsible innovation. This role offers a unique opportunity to contribute to a high-impact, workforce-aligned credential that bridges STEM expertise with emerging AI capabilities. What You Will Do: Evaluate learner submissions of the AI Workflow Project, which include a real-world STEM challenge, an AI-powered solution, and a complete 5-step workflow design. Apply structured rubrics to assess mastery of skills such as supervised learning, data preprocessing, model selection, and interpretability. Participate in calibration exercises with fellow graders (if needed) to ensure consistency in evaluating technical artifacts and conceptual reasoning. Maintain confidentiality and objectivity throughout the grading process What You Bring: Bachelor’s degree required; Master’s degree preferred in Computer Science, Data Science, Engineering, or related STEM disciplines. Subject-matter expertise in foundational AI concepts, including machine learning, data analysis, and ethical considerations in AI deployment. Experience in academic assessment, workforce development, or digital learning preferred. Familiarity with learning management systems (Moodle preferred), online credentialing platforms, and collaborative grading workflows. Professional, learner-centered approach with a commitment to academic integrity and continuous improvement. Proficient in rubric-based assessment and competency validation, especially for technical and project-based submissions. Strong attention to detail and ability to maintain consistency across diverse submissions. Excellent written communication skills for delivering constructive, learner-focused feedback. Comfortable working in asynchronous learning environments and using digital platforms. Adaptability in managing multiple grading tasks within deadlines. Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Capital-Project-Manager_R0013783-1": {
      "title": "Capital Project Manager",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-16\nFull time\n\nCapital Project Manager | Brandeis University | Waltham, MA Hiring range: $108k - $117k   About the role: In conjunction with and as directed by the Director of Design & Construction, the Capital Project Manager plans and executes small, high touch, and often high-priority projects, including small moves, furniture changes, minimal design and construction, site planning, utilities, and landscape improvements. Representing the University and the Design & Construction group, this position engages stakeholders such as occupant groups, Public Safety, and Facilities personnel and coordinates with architects, engineers, furniture vendors, construction managers, contractors, and city officials as required in connection with projects.   Key Responsibilities: Project Planning, Management and Execution Coordinates and manages small projects and/or various aspects of projects, including planning, budget development, design, construction, scheduling, and project closeout. Develops and coordinates project plans, specifications, sketches, and drawings as necessary. Works closely with stakeholders and University leaders to coordinate swing space, moves, furniture and equipment deliveries, and other project related activities.   Stakeholder and Client Communication Responsible for frequent communication with project stakeholders and occupant groups, including providing regular and weekly project updates. Serves as a primary point of contact for assigned projects and provides a high level of customer service to clients. Coordinates communication among university departments, Facilities personnel, consultants, contractors, vendors, and other project stakeholders to ensure projects remain aligned with expectations and operational needs.   Design, Construction and Site Coordination Coordinates with architects, engineers, contractors, construction managers, furniture vendors, and other consultants throughout the project lifecycle. Performs regular site inspections to ensure quality workmanship and adherence to project requirements. Leads project meetings and, when a construction manager is involved, attends construction coordination meetings and reports project status and issues to the Associate Director. Coordinates responses to requests for information from external contractors and Brandeis Facilities Services personnel regarding existing conditions, active projects, and completed work.   Budget and Financial Management Manages capital project budgets and tracks project expenditures against approved budgets. Develops project cost estimates and provides periodic updates on budget status. Maintains appropriate financial documentation, including change orders and payment requests. Obtains bids from multiple contractors or vendors when necessary and coordinates purchase orders and other financial requirements associated with assigned projects.   Project Documentation and Records Management   Maintains accurate and complete project documentation, including meeting minutes, financial records, change orders, purchase orders, plans, specifications, and other project-related records. Assists in maintaining files for as-built drawings and specifications for university buildings and related systems. Ensures project documentation is organized and available to support ongoing Facilities operations and future planning.   Codes, Permitting and Regulatory Coordination Supports compliance with applicable federal, state, local, and International Building Code requirements and permitting processes. Coordinates with appropriate city officials and regulatory agencies as required to support project approvals and ensure projects are executed in accordance with applicable codes, regulations, and University requirements.   Education & Work Experience: Bachelor’s degree; in some cases, commensurate experience may be considered in lieu of education. 3-5 years of related work experience with 2-3 years of project management experience.    Additional Qualifications: Familiarity with legal contract language especially negotiating terms, applying appropriate contract forms per project criteria, extra-contract legal understanding (labor, union, etc.). Ability to read, understand, interpret, and formulate plans/specifications and cost estimates. Knowledge or experience in developing and tracking budgets and schedules. Strong verbal and written communication, and collaboration skills. Background with federal, local and IBC codes/regulations and permitting processes. Strong problem-solving skills. Experience managing projects involving teams of design consultants, engineers and contractors.   Preferred Qualifications: Bachelor's degree in architecture, engineering, construction, or planning. Experience in a higher education setting. Knowledge of computer assisted design/drafting (Inventor, AutoCAD) equipment and software to develop and read designs. Experience managing projects utilizing project management software. Holds a Massachusetts Construction Supervisor License. Needs to be able to inspect work of others outside in varying weather conditions. Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Per-Diem-Staff-Therapist_R0013774-1": {
      "title": "Per Diem Staff Therapist",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-26\nPart time\n\nPer Diem Staff Therapist - Embedded Community Therapist, Brandeis Hillel (Temporary) Brandeis University Counseling Center | Waltham, MA $75/hour | 7–21 hours per week | Through May 15 Are you passionate about supporting college student mental health in a dynamic, diverse campus community? The Brandeis Counseling Center is seeking a Staff Therapist - Embedded Community Therapist, Brandeis Hillel to join our team on a temporary basis to help meet student care needs this spring. In this role, you will provide culturally responsive, trauma-informed, and strength-based mental health care to undergraduate and graduate students, primarily located at the Brandeis Hillel. You will work with a collaborative team of colleagues while supporting students through individual therapy, group treatment, crisis support, and community outreach. This is an excellent opportunity for clinicians who enjoy short-term, high-impact work in a university counseling setting and who value inclusive, identity-affirming care. What You Will Do Provide individual and group therapy for college students Conduct assessments, triage, and crisis intervention Deliver trauma-informed and culturally adaptive care Participate in community outreach, workshops, and consultation Collaborate with campus partners to support student wellbeing Contribute to team meetings, documentation, and program initiatives Qualifications Master’s degree in Social Work or Mental Health Counseling (or PhD/PsyD in a related field) LICSW or LMHC license in Massachusetts Experience providing therapy to young adults or college students preferred If you are looking for meaningful, flexible clinical work supporting students during a critical time in the academic year, we encourage you to apply. Apply today and help support student mental health at Brandeis. Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Lecturer-in-the-University-Writing-Program_R0013814": {
      "title": "Lecturer in the University Writing Program",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-26\nPart time\n\nThe University Writing Program at Brandeis University invites applications for a full-time Lecturer position beginning in Fall 2027 to teach composition courses and provide English Language Learning (ELL) support to first-year students as needed. The successful candidate will teach a 3/3 load of first-year writing courses, primarily Composition Seminars (CSEM). Additional responsibilities may include supporting developing writers, English language learners, and multilingual students through tutoring, small-group instruction, and other language-support initiatives, with course releases provided as appropriate. This is a renewable one-year appointment, subject to programmatic needs and budgetary approval. Authorized salary for this position is $68,000-$70,000, commensurate with experience. Candidates should demonstrate a strong commitment to excellence in undergraduate writing instruction and hold a Ph.D. in English, Rhetoric and Composition, or a related field. Preference will be given to candidates with significant experience in teaching first-year composition and supporting first-year students through individualized writing instruction, tutoring, academic coaching, and related student success initiatives. Applicants should submit: A cover letter addressing their qualifications and interest in the position An up-to-date CV A concise statement describing their experience working with developing writers, English language learners, or multilingual learners and their involvement in student success interventions A teaching portfolio including a sample syllabus for a composition course and selected evidence of teaching effectiveness, such as assignments, instructional materials, or student feedback Names and contact information for two professional references. Brandeis will contact these individuals confidentially for candidates who advance in the search process. For full consideration, applications should be submitted by November 15, 2026. Review of applications will continue until the position is filled. Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Assistant-Registrar--Records-and-Reporting_R0013802-1": {
      "title": "Assistant Registrar, Records and Reporting",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-23\nFull time\n\nBrandeis University is delighted to announce a career opportunity as the Assistant Registrar, Records and Reporting in Enrollment Management. At Brandeis, we offer a competitive benefits and compensation package which includes medical, dental and life insurances.  If you are looking to advance your career through educational opportunities, Brandeis offers tuition assistance for employees, dependents and spouses.  Our 403b retirement savings plan includes a generous match.   The Assistant Registrar, Records and Reporting is responsible for internal and external reporting: IPEDS, CDS, and a wide range of ad-hoc requests from administrative offices across the university. Manages critical functions associated with transcripts, degree and enrollment verification production and the full range of business processes in the student information system (Workday Student). Manages all undergraduate and graduate change of status requests (e.g. withdrawal, leave of absence, readmission) and overall maintenance of student records. Assists the University Registrar with the administration of final exams. Oversees one staff position responsible for transfer credit evaluation, veteran’s benefits, and supporting internal surveys.   The hiring range for this position is $62.8k - $67k. This is a hybrid position; minimum of 2 days per week in person. To be considered for this position please submit a cover letter and resume.   Responsibilities:   Oversees internal and external reporting for the Registrar’s Office, including IPEDS, CDS, and all ad-hoc requests from university administrators and offices. Responsible for the creation and maintenance of reports and queries in the student records system (Workday Student). Assists with the set-up and maintenance of the student records system’s configuration and policies. Maintains integrations with upstream and down-stream systems (including Slate and the National Student Clearinghouse). Responsible for hiring and managing final exam proctors. Manages day-to-day student records functions including updating student records to reflect changes in status, standing and program of study. Responsible for duplicate student records, student cohorts, student holds and batch data loads (e.g. AP, holds, course registrations, etc). Manages and implements data integrity standards relative to Student Records. May be responsible for the training and coordination of the business practices related to data management within Student Records both internally and with other departments on campus. Works with IT to maintain business process definitions and set-up in Workday student. Assists the University Registrar with the administration and oversight of final exams.   Assists with all registration and Commencement activities, maintenance of the office website, student walk-in support, general office inquiries, and other duties as assigned.   Education and Work Experience: BA/BS required. M.A./M.S. preferred. 3-5 years of work-related experience including 1-3 years of supervisory/management experience. Requirements: 3-5 years of experience working with centralized databases, with preference for knowledge of student administration information systems. Must be fluent with MS Office applications~ including advanced Excel; familiarity with HTML editors and HTML source code/web publishing applications. Strong oral and written communication skills, and strong organization and planning skills. Ability to manage and effectively interact with campus partners. Requires self-management and attention to detail. Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Horticultural-Specialist_R0013788-1": {
      "title": "Horticultural Specialist",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-23\nFull time\n\nUnder the direction of the Manager, Grounds & Fleet, the Horticultural Specialist maintains campus grounds, including lawn areas, mulch beds, athletics fields, sidewalks and roadways. Primarily focuses on the care and maintenance of perennials and nursery stock. Applies pesticides and fertilizers to campus. Removes snow and ice from campus sidewalks and roadways. Participates in the trash, recycling and compost operation on campus. Must have an extensive knowledge of grounds keeping of various types of land to include pesticides and irrigation. This position is a Local 615, 32BJ Service Employees International Union (SEIU) position.   The hourly rate for this position is $37.29 per hour, 40 hours per week. The schedule is Monday – Friday 7AM – 3PM   Key Responsibilities: Horticultural Specialist Functions: Must have sufficient knowledge of landscaping, lawn care, irrigation, and pesticides. Must have an extensive knowledge of plant material and pruning techniques and timing. Must be able to recommend small horticultural designs and execute the plan for planters and beds Must have a strong passion for campus beautification. Instruct and direct fellow employees on horticultural techniques when assigned. Mix and apply pesticides, fertilizers, lime and other plant health products to lawns, athletic fields and garden beds in accordance with the product label. Keep records, write reports, and identify grounds materials. Schedule campus wide irrigation system. Utilize Equipment requiring a MA hoisting license. Utilize front end loaders to remove and treat snow and ice from all areas on campus. Main Functions: Reports any issues to the appropriate supervisor. Maintain campus lawns utilizing both power tools and equipment as well as hand tools and equipment. Troubleshoot and repair campus wide irrigation system. Maintain athletic areas including but not limited to baseball field, softball field, soccer field, artificial turf and tennis courts. Interpret and stake out plans based on drawings and designs. Prune trees and shrubs utilizing hand and power tools. Plant trees, shrubs, perennials, annuals and bulbs. Utilize trucks and utility vehicles with plows and sanders, as well as front end loaders to remove and treat snow and ice from all areas on campus. Install asphalt, cold patch, concrete, cement and other construction materials. Perform trash, recycling and litter pickup. Cleans and maintains outdoor furniture. Removes outdoor graffiti including exteriors of buildings and pavement. Removes unapproved signs when directed by the Manager. Assist in moving furniture or equipment as assigned. Refuel emergency generators throughout campus. Must be able to work alone with minimal supervision. General Functions : Follow proper safety procedures and observe all safety rules and regulations including OSHA. Reports exterior campus damage, destruction, or repairs needed in assigned work areas. Respond to emergency call-ins as needed. Read, write, understand, and carry out oral and written instructions in English. Participates in training to keep and acquire knowledge, skills, and abilities in order to remain current with standard operational practices and revised standards. Establish and maintain a positive cooperative working relationship with supervisors, fellow workers, department heads, and other members of the campus community. Work in an environment, primarily outdoors, subject to frequent changing climate conditions, priorities and deadlines. Drive a University Vehicle. *This list is representative of those duties and responsibilities that are required of this position. However, the list is not to be considered as all inclusive. Job Qualifications and Requirements: Physical/Environmental Demands: Prolonged periods of standing and walking. Work will be performed outside during all New England seasons. Manually lift up to 60 pounds. Heavier material or equipment is lifted using other methods. Drive a University Vehicle. Education, Licenses and Work Experience: High School Diploma or GED required. Associate’s degree in landscaping, horticulture, or land management preferred. Valid and current United States Driver’s License required. Massachusetts Hoisting License 1C/2A required. Massachusetts Pesticide Applicator’s License required. DOT Medical Certificate. 4 years of related experience in a horticultural field. Additional Requirements/Qualifications: Frequent use of verbal skills and written skills. May explain and offer guidance on routine horticultural procedures. Must have an extensive understanding of proper perennial, tree and shrub care. Must have thorough understand irrigation system uses and repair Must have a basic understanding of mowing and trimming practices. Must have basic athletic field maintenance and setup knowledge. Must be able to use hand and power tools. Has frequent contact with other employees, professional staff and/or external contacts to give or obtain information or deliver service. Results can affect our own functional area. Must have the ability to follow instructions from supervisors or senior managers. Decisions may affect a work unit or area within a department. May contribute to business and operational decisions that affect the department. Make decisions in lieu of the Grounds Manager when absent. Problems are varied, requiring analysis or interpretation of the situation. Problems are solved using knowledge and skills, general precedents, and practices. Results are defined. Determines how to accomplish results with little guidance. Supervision/manager provides broad guidance and overall direction. Must be able to work within Facilities Services budget. Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Staff-Therapist--all-levels-_R0013805-1": {
      "title": "Staff Therapist (all levels)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-22\nFull time\n\nLocation: Brandeis University – Waltham, MA Staff Therapist - Wide-ranging clinical expertise and cultural responsiveness to support students from a variety of backgrounds, identities, and experiences. At Brandeis University, we believe mental health is foundational to student success. Our Brandeis Counseling Center (BCC) plays a vital role in nurturing the emotional well-being of a dynamic, richly varied student population, and passionate student body. We are looking for a collaborative and compassionate therapist to join our team as Staff Therapist — Someone who brings wide-ranging clinical expertise and cultural responsiveness to support students from a variety of backgrounds, identities, and experiences. What You will Do As a Staff Therapist providing clinical care to students from a variety of backgrounds, identities, and experiences, you will: Support student wellness : Deliver short-term, solution-focused individual therapy, crisis intervention, and case management as needed. Participate in after-hours, on-call rotation. Foster learning : Conduct outreach programs and workshops to promote mental health awareness to students and staff. Collaborate across campus : Work closely with student-facing departments within the Student Affairs Division, as well as our campus partners at the Intercultural Center, Gender and Sexuality Center, Academic Services, Posse, Upward Bound, and other initiatives supporting students broadly to ensure holistic student support. Advance access : Support our mission to be a center that provides access to a wide range of students. Provide training and support: Offer training, technical assistance, consultation, and support to colleagues across campus and within the Brandeis Counseling Center. Support the Training Program through supervision of graduate trainees and didactic training seminars.  What You Bring Current licensure in the state of Massachusetts as a licensed therapist or expectation that candidate is licensed-eligible within 12 months of hire. Has experience providing counseling services in an outpatient setting.  Demonstrated ability to provide culturally responsive care and work effectively with students from a broad range of backgrounds, identities, and communities. A warm, team-oriented approach and genuine enthusiasm for being visible and involved on campus.  Our team is often present running groups, connecting with students at outreach events, and participating in mental health training for campus staff.  The Schedule Year-round, on-campus role (35 hours/week, business hours) Why Work at Brandeis? Be part of a mission-driven team that cares deeply about community, inclusivity, and student success. Join a supportive, collaborative environment where your ideas are valued and your expertise makes a real difference. Engage in meaningful professional development through working groups, trainings, and peer consultation. Enjoy fantastic benefits , including: Comprehensive health and wellness benefits Generous paid time off and holidays Free on-campus parking Free gym membership The hiring range for this position is $61,100 - $99,400. (level and salary are commensurate with experience). Multiple positions available. Ready to join a team that is reimagining campus mental health?   Apply today by submitting your cover letter and resume. Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Public-Services-Coordinator_R0013795-1": {
      "title": "Public Services Coordinator",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-21\nFull time\n\nBrandeis University is looking for Public Services Coordinator in the Library Services. Reporting to the Director, Access Services, the Public Services Coordinator is responsible for providing high quality, user-focused experiences to library users at Brandeis. Coordinates daily operations of front-line library services, including borrowing transactions, information assistance, stacks maintenance, equipment use, data collection, building operations, and student employee oversight. The Public Services Coordinator may work during regular hours or during evening or weekend hours and may participate in holiday and inclement weather coverage as required. This is a union position. Employees working in this position are covered by the collective bargaining agreement between the University and Service Employees International Union (SEIU) Local 888.  Provisions related to wages, benefits and other terms of employment are contained in the agreement. The hourly payrate for the position is $29.56-$33.51/hr. The schedule for the job is Tuesday-Saturday, 2 p.m. to 10 p.m. For full consideration, please submit your resume and cover letter. We encourage applicants to submit their applications by November 15, 2026, for priority consideration. Essential Functions: Supervises services and operations at the Information & Borrowing desk and in the Commons that result in successful patron-focused experiences. Coordinates and assists with user interactions including handling circulation functions, equipment and informational assistance, and oversight of building operations. Communicates and enforces policies, participates in data collection and reporting, and maintains routine oversight of building operations and safety protocols to ensure the library maintains a safe and welcoming environment for users. Answers user questions via phone and email. Works with sensitive and confidential information and fine collection. Works collaboratively to develop improvements to policies and procedures resulting in better user experience. Assists with other projects and initiatives of the Department and the Library broadly. 35% Manages, develops, and enhances the library's equipment loan program through all aspects of its lifecycle. Assists in long-term planning for the equipment loan program and uses quantitative and qualitative data to inform these decisions. Directly responsible for day to day management of the equipment inventory, including troubleshooting issues, purchasing new and replacement items, tracking inventory, and labeling and general preparation for circulation. Works regularly with internal and external stakeholders on issues and requests for purchase related to equipment lending, including working with outside equipment vendors. Responsible for student and staff training and documentation surrounding equipment. Works with the Manager of Public Services to evaluate and adjust equipment policies and procedures. Keeps abreast of current trends and issues relating to equipment lending as well as new technology and their application in academic libraries. 15% Manages, plans, and evaluates the operations of equipment in the Library Commons, including the scanners and microfilm machines. Facilitates access and use of desktop computing, general maintenance, and software updates through ongoing communication with IT Clusters. Serves as the library’s contact for Commons’ equipment vendors. Facilitates access and use of the Public Services Department and Faculty/Staff Printers through contact and collaboration with our vendor, Ricoh. Works collaboratively with Library and ITS stakeholders to improve and promote Commons services, including printing, scanning, and desktop computing. Works with the Manager of Public Services on long-term planning for the Commons space and equipment loan program. 15% Participates in hiring, training, scheduling, and supervision of student staff. Works with the Public Services team to screen candidates and make hiring decisions. Assists with student employee training and staff information sessions. Works collaboratively with the Public Services team to review, evaluate, and revise student employee training and documentation. Coaches students when working on procedures and customer service. Assists with providing semesterly student evaluations and documents performance issues as needed. 15 % Manages building access and security during scheduled work hours, including evenings and weekends. Communicates and ensures respectful enforcement of policies, procedures, and rules while ensuring strong customer service and safety of users and staff. Understands emergency procedures and works closely with Public Safety, acting as the first responder to issues within the building. 10% Other duties as assigned. 10% Physical/Environmental Demands: Moderate physical activity such as sitting, standing, walking, kneeling, and lifting an average of 25 lbs. Requirements Education: B.A./B.S. or equivalent required. Background in an academic library environment or librarianship preferred. Familiarity with automated library system or similar system preferred. Work Experience: 1-3 years Ability to thoughtfully work with patrons and colleagues to resolve issues, including sensitive situations The Public Services Coordinator is primarily an on-site position. Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Orientation-Specialist_R0013799-1": {
      "title": "Orientation Specialist",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-20\nFull time\n\nEngage with students through initiatives for incoming first year and transfer students . Build leaders. Strengthen communities. At Brandeis University, we believe education extends far beyond the classroom. We are seeking an energetic, creative, and collaborative Orientation Specialist who will have oversight of the Orientation Leader selection process, support summer Orientation program development, and contributes to the overall retention and success initiatives for incoming first year and transfer students. The hiring range for this position is $53,200 - $61,700. This is a live-on position and will serve as part of the duty rotation for the Department of Community Living and will participate in cross-training opportunities to support this work. What you will do: Oversee recruitment, selection, and training for approximately 50 student Orientation Leaders. Coordinate Move-In Crew program which includes recruiting and managing student volunteers to assist with first year move-in to the residence halls.  Support orientation and first year program development and implementation including liaising with vendors, University Events, and campus partners.  Selects, trains, advises, and evaluates 6-8 First Year Ambassadors and Transfer Ambassadors who work throughout the academic year to provide outreach, programming and support to new students.  Serve as part of the Community Living Area Coordinator team, sharing in duty and on-call responsibilities.  Support strategic planning, development of leadership programs, and student advising. Manage related program budgets. Support departmental programs and events and participate in divisional/university committees and initiatives as applicable.  Qualifications & Additional Information: BA/BS required/M.A./M.S. preferred plus 1-3 years’ experience Driver's license preferred to drive vans, community living vehicles, and golf carts; nights and weekend work required for community living and orientation programming. Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Lecturer-in-Mathematics--Introduction-to-STEM-Skills-_R0013800": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Mathematics (Introduction to STEM Skills)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-20\nPart time\n\nThe Department of Mathematics at Brandeis University seeks an instructor to teach two sections (running sequentially) of MATH 2f Introduction to STEM Skills, a two-credit course in Spring 2027. The course is intended to provide support to students who do not qualify to take a calculus class or need extra support to succeed in these classes. It helps students build the basic mathematical skills they will need in STEM courses, hone their problem-solving skills, and develop and leverage a learning community at Brandeis. There are no pre-requisite for this class. The class meets Monday and Wednesday, 2:30pm – 3:50pm. Candidates with a MA or a PhD in mathematics or education , and teaching experience in these domains, will be given preference. Interested individuals should submit a cover letter, CV, a draft course syllabus, and names and contact information for two references. The deadline for first consideration is November 1, 2026. This appointment is to a position that is in a collective bargaining unit represented by SEIU Local 509.   Compensation for this assignment is $8764.20. Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Senior-Enrollment-Operations-Coordinator_R0013786-1": {
      "title": "Senior Enrollment Operations Coordinator",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-19\nFull time\n\nBrandeis University is delighted to announce a career opportunity as the Senior Enrollment Operations Coordinator. At Brandeis, we offer a competitive benefits and compensation package which includes medical, dental and life insurances.  If you are looking to advance your career through educational opportunities, Brandeis offers tuition assistance for employees, dependents and spouses.  Our 403b retirement savings plan includes a generous match. The Senior Enrollment Operations Coordinator ensures accurate and timely processing of application materials and supports prospective students through professional communication across multiple channels. Leveraging the Slate CRM, they manage operational email and SMS campaigns, supervise a team of operational student workers, and maintain data integrity. This role also provides essential logistical and operational support for both the undergraduate and graduate admissions office, contributing to daily operations, recruitment events, and overarching enrollment strategies. The hiring range for this position is $51k - $54k. This is a hybrid position.   Key Responsibilities: Application Workflow and Service Support Manage the intake and matching of incoming application materials with student records in Slate CRM. Ensure student files are complete and ready for review by counselors and recruiters, conducting outreach and follow-up to applicants as necessary. Provide professional and timely customer service via email, phone, and SMS to prospective students, current applicants, families, and school counselors. Hire, train, and supervise a team of operational student workers who support frontline communications, mail handling, and data entry. Data Integrity and Systems Hygiene Execute routine data hygiene protocols, including auditing and consolidating duplicate records within the Slate CRM. Ensure that contact cards and other student leads received by counseling staff are entered into the CRM in a timely and accurate manner. Monitor data entry processes performed by student staff to ensure accuracy and efficiency. Operational Communications Manage and execute the operational communications calendar, including targeted email and SMS campaigns. Collaborate closely with the Enrollment Marketing team to ensure communications align with broader recruitment goals and university branding . General Office Support Coordinate day-to-day operational logistics, including mail processing, secure document handling, and ordering and organizing office supplies. Assist with preparing materials and staffing events as needed. Education and Work Experience: BA/BS required. 1+ years relevant professional experience. What you need to succeed: Familiarity with CRM systems, preferably Technolutions Slate. Basic proficiency in Excel and data management. Ability to train and supervise student staff while managing multiple priorities in a fast-paced environment. Strong organizational, communication, and customer service skills. Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Temporary-Assistant-Tennis-Coach--Men-s-Tennis_R0013821-1": {
      "title": "Temporary Assistant Tennis Coach, Men's Tennis",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-28\nPart time\n\nThe Temporary Assistant Coach will assist the head coach in running a successful men's tennis program which competes as a NCAA Division III program and as a member of the University Athletic Association (UAA). This includes practice and competition coaching, recruiting student-athletes at a national level, design and implementation of competitive strategies and advising and mentoring our student-athletes. The assistant must understand and promote the mission, goals, values and educational ideals of Brandeis University, as well as the mission of the Department of Athletics, Physical Education and Recreation. This is a part time position for 1/16/26 - 5/31/27. The salary for this position is $4,000. Education & Work Experience:  BA/BS required 1-3 years of coaching experience is preferred Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Adjunct-Instructor-in-Software-Development-in-Python_R0013822": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor in Software Development in Python",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-28\nPart time\n\nBrandeis University’s Rabb School is seeking an Adjunct Faculty member for RSEG 111: Software Development in Python, for the 2027 Spring 1 session. This 3-credit asynchronous online course is an 8-week requirement for the Master of Science in Software Engineering Software Engineering Artificial Intelligence Track and Software Engineering with Artificial Intelligence Online Master's Certificate . Course Description: This course involves software development, scientific problem-solving and machine learning approaches using Python. Topics include procedures and functions, iteration, recursion, arrays and vectors, strings, an operational model of procedure and function calls, algorithms, exceptions, object-oriented programming, and GUIs (graphical user interfaces). Students will practice scientific programming and machine learning approaches using libraries such as SciPy, NumPy, Keras or Pytorch. Core Course Responsibilities Summary Course Logistics and Facilitation : Focuses on the organized and timely rollout of course content, maintaining consistent communication through weekly announcements, and ensuring all instructional activities occur within university-approved digital platforms. Instructor Presence and Engagement : Centers on building an active teaching persona by hosting live introductory sessions, facilitating weekly academic discourse in forums, and maintaining regular availability for student consultation. Individual Feedback and Grading : Emphasizes the professional obligation to provide transparent, rubric-based evaluations and supportive commentary on student work within a standardized weekly timeframe. Professional Conduct and Standards : Requires adherence to university communication protocols, the promotion of respectful online \"netiquette,\" and ensuring the course meets accessibility and technical visibility standards before and during the term. Qualifications: Required: Master’s degree (Doctorate preferred) in Computer Science or in a relevant discipline At least 1 year of teaching or training experience (preferably online/asynchronous) Proficiency with LMS platforms and digital authoring tools Familiarity with analytical tools, collaborative platforms, and interdisciplinary teamwork Strong communication, organization, and independent work skills Preferred: Experience teaching or developing graduate-level online courses Prior online teaching experience at the graduate level  Knowledge of global learner personas and culturally responsive pedagogy Familiarity with Moodle LMS and digital authoring tools (e.g., H5P) Application Process: Interested candidates should submit: A cover letter highlighting relevant qualifications and teaching experience. A current CV or resume. Contact information for three professional references. Application review begins October 29, 2026, though we will continue to accept submissions on an ongoing basis. This appointment is to a position that is in a collective bargaining unit represented by SEIU Local 509. Compensation for this position is $6573.15 Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Part-time-Technical-Support-Specialist_R0013809-1": {
      "title": "Part-time Technical Support Specialist",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-28\nPart time\n\nBrandeis University is looking for a Part-time Technical Support Specialist. The main purpose of this half-time (0.5 FTE) job is to expand what the Brandeis Light Microscopy Core Facility can do for its users. The main focus is assisted-use imaging : running experiments on behalf of external academic and industry users, and on behalf of Brandeis researchers. Working under the direction of the facility manager, the employee will run instruments, train and support users at basic to intermediate levels, and do routine maintenance and quality control. The facility’s instruments include widefield, confocal, spinning disk, 2-photon, STED super-resolution and light-sheet microscopes. The employee will help with day-to-day facility operations, including booking accounts, training requests, usage records, and operational spreadsheets and reports. The employee will also join workshops, outreach and professional development activities, so that the facility stays rigorous, reproducible and responsive to a growing academic and industry user base. Job Description Performs assisted-use imaging for external academic and industry users and for Brandeis researchers, under the direction of the facility manager. This includes sample handling, image acquisition on the facility’s microscopes, first-pass image processing, and organized, documented data delivery. Gives users timely, high-quality results so that they keep coming back. Keeps records of the work done for each project - 35% Gives consultation, basic to intermediate training, and technical support to facility users across a range of light-microscopy techniques. Demonstrates standard operating procedures (SOPs) following facility guidelines. Helps users with image acquisition and introductory image processing and analysis workflows (e.g., ImageJ/Fiji) - 25% Performs routine maintenance, quality-control checks and performance assessments on facility instruments, including widefield, confocal, spinning disk, 2-photon, STED super-resolution and light-sheet systems. Troubleshoots equipment problems and works with instrument manufacturers and their service engineers on support and repairs, escalating to the facility manager as needed. Helps maintain SOPs that ensure data quality, rigor and reproducibility. May supervise student workers doing routine maintenance and facility tasks: assigns and checks their work, and trains them on procedures - 20% Helps with day-to-day facility operations: managing user booking accounts, processing training requests, keeping usage and training records, and maintaining the facility’s operational spreadsheets and periodic reports. Helps ensure that staff and users follow laboratory health, safety and risk-assessment protocols - 15% Takes part in internal and external professional development, technical workshops and imaging-network events. Supports outreach, facility tours and workshops for academic and industry users, and cross-trains with the facility manage- 5% This is a part-time position working 17.5 hours a week. The salary range for the position is $32k-$40k (for half-time). Job Requirements: Bachelor's degree in STEM research field is required. Master's degree is preferred. solid theoretical and practical knowledge of light microscopy, including widefield and confocal imaging is required. Experience with at least one advanced modality (e.g., super-resolution, light-sheet or 2-photon) is preferred. Should be familiar with biological sample preparation, image-acquisition strategies and image analysis software (e.g., ImageJ/Fiji). Strong communication, teaching and interpersonal skills with a service-oriented mindset. Strong problem-solving and organizational skills, attention to research ethics and data integrity, Ability to support many concurrent projects for a diverse academic and industry user base. Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Temporary-Assistant-Tennis-Coach--Women-s-Tennis_R0013818-1": {
      "title": "Temporary Assistant Tennis Coach, Women's Tennis",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-28\nPart time\n\nThe Temporary Assistant Coach will assist the head coach in running a successful women's tennis program which competes as a NCAA Division III program and as a member of the University Athletic Association (UAA). This includes practice and competition coaching, recruiting student-athletes at a national level, design and implementation of competitive strategies and advising and mentoring our student-athletes. The assistant must understand and promote the mission, goals, values and educational ideals of Brandeis University, as well as the mission of the Department of Athletics, Physical Education and Recreation. This is a part time position for 1/16/26 - 5/31/27. The hiring range for this position is $4,000-$4,500. Education & Work Experience:  BA/BS required 1-3 years of coaching experience is preferred Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Director--Admin-Ops--School-of-Science--Engineering---Technology_R0013808-1": {
      "title": "Director, Admin/Ops, School of Science, Engineering & Technology",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-27\nFull time\n\nThe Director, Admin/Ops SET supports the School of Science, Engineering & Technology within the university’s evolving structure. This position provides operational leadership, administrative oversight, and strategic planning for this assigned school. The Director reports to the Academic Dean of the respective school, works closely with the Provost’s Office and is a liaison to central university offices including Human Resources, Capital Projects, Finance, Information Technology Services, Space Planning, Research, Office Graduate Affairs, Undergraduate Affairs, Academic Affairs, and Communications.   This position will supervise administrative staff, lead special projects, and collaborate with stakeholders across academic and administrative units to ensure the smooth functioning of school operations; and will work in partnership and coordination with the Provost's Office to ensure academy-wide alignment and consistency.   Managerial Scope: This position directly and indirectly manages an organization of 25 -30 employees in the following functions and disciplines, in collaboration with central offices: Academic Administration, Business Administration, Graduate Academic Administration, Communications & Events, and Scientific Computing (includes Applications). The staff members in the research facilities are also supported by the SET Dean and Director. The salary range for the position is $130.7-$148k   Job Description Administrative and Operational Oversight – 45% Oversee daily administrative operations, including research administration, academic administration, budgets and finance, space coordination, facilities coordination, and workflow efficiency. Supervise and mentor school-level administrative staff and coordinators and liaise with cross-functional units. This position directly and indirectly manages an organization of 25-30 employees in the following functions and disciplines: Academic Administration (including Graduate Guidance), Communications & Events, Business Administration[CF1] Develop structures, business policies and processes and implement administrative policies in collaboration with Academic Dean and consistent with University policies and processes. Consult with Provost's Office and central HR on staff recruitment requests. Serve as primary school liaison to central HR Compensation on compensation and job description requests. Ensure University staff hiring processes and policies are followed; coordinate with HR and Provost's Office on interviewing and onboarding new staff. Coordinate logistics for staff performance review and salary planning processes for assigned school, in partnership with the Associate Dean. Foster an inclusive and high-performing administrative staff culture. Serve on committees as needed.   Strategic Leadership & Planning – 35% Support the school and collaborate with the Academic Dean in administrative planning, operational decision-making, and resource prioritization. Lead development and implementation of process improvements, administrative realignments, and change management strategies. Represent school on institutional committees and project teams (e.g., Workday governance, HR process design). Manage administrative budgets and present reports to the Dean. Manage grant, gifts and endowment funds, including reporting systems to allow faculty to track budgets and spending. Oversee core facilities budgets, working with faculty and facility Directors. Work in close collaboration with the Provost Finance Team throughout the fiscal year. Collaborate with Provost's Office and other school Directors to implement University policies across all schools. Act as high-level staff liaison with Chairs. Coordinate with the Provost Office on capital planning and space needs. Partner with Faculty and Academic Affairs on faculty hiring and curricular planning, as needed.   Special Projects, Research and Academic Operations – 15% Manage academic support functions, such as awards and Commencement activities. Manage research support functions, such as cost sharing and institutional support letters, in collaboration with the Academic Dean and the Provost Office. Work with the Vice Provost of Research to ensure regulatory compliance with state and Federal regulations. Lead school-level initiatives and projects as assigned by the Academic Dean. Provide operational support for research, academic programs, and student affairs in collaboration with faculty and administrative colleagues, including oversight of support for undergraduate research.   Communication and Stakeholder Engagement – 5% Maintain consistent communication with school staff, academic chairs, and central university offices. Ensure timely dissemination of operational updates, policy changes, and process improvements. Coordinate with Provost's Office on opportunities for academy-wide communication and engagement.   Requirements:   BA/BS required, preferably in a science or engineering field. Master or doctoral degree in higher education, public administration, science field, or related field preferred. Minimum 8 years of progressively responsible administrative experience in a complex organization required. At least 10 years is preferred. At least 5 years of supervisory or management experience required. Experience in higher education, nonprofit management, scientific operations, or academic operations preferred. Demonstrated ability to lead administrative teams and implement systems that improve operations. Strong leadership and interpersonal skills; able to build trust and work collaboratively across departments and schools. Experience with HR processes, recruitment, and performance management. Experience with research administration. Strong organizational and communication skills; ability to manage multiple priorities. Proficiency with Microsoft Office, Excel, and administrative systems (Workday or similar platforms preferred). Experience with space management or capital project coordination is helpful but not required. Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Research-Associate-I_R0013820-1": {
      "title": "Research Associate I",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-29\nFull time\n\nBrandeis University seeks to hire a Research Associate I who will contribute substantively to several major community-based projects at the Institute of Behavioral Health (IBH). This person will work closely with the principal investigator (Traci Green). The Research Associate I will add to the research team's capacity to create reproducible research results through project and budget oversight, meticulous documentation of data files, data processes, and interrelationships of multiple complex data sources. This person will support a wide range of research efforts that include quantitative and qualitative analysis, primary data collection, community engaged research, engaging appropriate stakeholders and resources; leading strong internal and external team communications; drafting progress reports and other public-facing materials; overseeing IRB communications; and tracking project budgets.  This is a full-time grant funded position. The hiring range is $51,300 - $65,000. Job Duties: Prepare or assist with presentations for conferences, local government, and community partner meetings; prepare or assist with preparation of manuscripts and other project materials. 10% Oversee and coordinate project activities, review budgets and staffing, communicate with Institutional Review Board (IRB) 25% Responsible for research deliverables including quantitative and qualitative analysis. 10% Curate datasets and create communication tools for accessing datasets. 15% Participate in study data collection efforts. 20% Leading strong internal and external communication efforts, coordinating completion of research activities. 20% Requirements: Bachelor's degree required, Masters degree in health/behavioral health interest preferred 1-3 years relevant work experience Experience with NVivo (or equivalent qualitative analysis software), R or SAS required. This position will be exclusively represented by the Service Employees International Union (SEIU), Local 888 for purposes of wages, hours and other terms and conditions of employment. Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    },
    "https://brandeis.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Jobs/job/Brandeis---Waltham-Campus/Lecturer-in-Music--Hip-Hop-and-Popular-Music-Production-_R0013827": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Music (Hip Hop and Popular Music Production)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-30\nPart time\n\nThe Departments of Music and African American Studies at Brandeis University seek an instructor for the course Hip Hop and Popular Music Production, a 4 credit course in Spring 2027. This course studies hip hop as both a technological history (turntables, synthesizers, drum machines, samplers) and a social history (migration, civil rights, deindustrialization, media consolidation, regional identity). Each week pairs a listening and reading unit with a hands-on production lab. The class meets for three hours on Fridays from 9:35am-12:25pm. Candidates with a Masters Degree or higher and at least five years of teaching and or music industry experience will be given preference. Interested individuals should submit a cover letter, CV, a draft course syllabus, and names and contact information for two references. The deadline for first consideration is November 1. This appointment is to a position that is in a collective bargaining unit represented by SEIU Local 509. The compensation for this course is $10,000. Pay Range Disclosure The University's pay ranges represent a good faith estimate of what Brandeis reasonably expects to pay for a position at the time of posting. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. Equal Opportunity Statement Brandeis University is an equal opportunity employer which does not discriminate against any applicant or employee on the basis of race, color, ancestry, religious creed, gender identity and expression, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, age, genetic information, disability, caste, military or veteran status or any other category protected by law (also known as membership in a \"protected class\")."
    }
  },
